window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2018-12-2", "source": "2018年12月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on how to balance job responsibilities and personal interests. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "Many people feel torn between the demands of their jobs and the hobbies they love, believing the two can\nnever coexist. In my view, however, work responsibilities and personal interests are not enemies; with a few\npractical habits, we can honor both.\nThe key lies in deliberate action rather than good intentions. First, I plan my week in advance, blocking out\nfixed hours for work tasks and reserving evenings for painting, so neither slips into the other's territory.\nSecond, I set clear boundaries by switching off work messages once I leave the office; in this way, my leisure\ntime stays truly mine. Finally, working efficiently during office hours frees up energy for what I enjoy, and as a\nresult I return to my desk refreshed rather than drained.\nBalance, therefore, is not a matter of choosing one over the other but of managing time and energy wisely. By\ntreating both duties and passions as parts of a full life, we can perform well at work and still grow the interests\nthat make us who we are."}, "translation": {"prompt": "中国越来越重视公共图书馆，并鼓励人们充分加以利用。新近公布的统计数字表明，中国的公共图书馆数量在逐年增长。许多图书馆通过翻新和扩建，为读者创造了更为安静、舒适的环境。大型公共图书馆不仅提供种类繁多的参考资料，而且定期举办讲座、展览等活动。近年来，也出现了许多数字图书馆，从而节省了存放图书所需的空间。一些图书馆还推出了自助服务系统，使读者借书还书更加方便，进一步满足了读者的需求。", "sample": "China is attaching greater importance to public libraries and encouraging people to make full use of them. The recently released statistics show that the number of public libraries in China is growing year by year. Many libraries have created a quieter and more comfortable environment for readers by renovating and expanding their buildings. Large public libraries not only provide a wide variety of reference materials but also regularly hold activities such as lectures and exhibitions. In recent years, many digital libraries have also emerged, thus saving the space needed for storing books. In addition, some libraries have introduced self-service systems, which make it more convenient for readers to borrow and return books and further meet their needs."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2018-12-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Surfing the Internet during class doesn't just steal focus from the educator; it also hurts students who're already struggling to  __26__  the material. A new study from Michigan State University, though, argues that all students—including high achievers—see a decline in performance when they browse the Internet during class for non-academic purposes.\nTo measure the effects of Internet-based distractions during class, researchers  __27__  500 students taking an introductory psychology class at Michigan State University. Researchers used ACT scores as a measure of intellectual  __28__  . Because previous research has shown that people with high intellectual abilities are better at  __29__  out distractions, researchers believed students with high ACT scores would not show a  __30__  decrease in performance due to their use of digital devices. But students who surfed the web during class did worse on their exams regardless of their ACT scores, suggesting that even the academically smartest students are harmed when they're distracted in class.\nCollege professors are increasingly  __31__  alarm bells about the effects smartphones, laptops, and tablets have on academic performance. One 2013 study of college students found that 80% of students use their phones or laptops during class, with the average student checking their digital device 11 times in a  __32__  class. A quarter of students report that their use of digital devices during class causes their grades to  __33__  .\nProfessors sometimes implement policies designed to  __34__  students' use of digital devices, and some instructors even confiscate(没收) tablets and phones. In a world where people are increasingly dependent on their phones, though, such strategies often fail. One international study found that 84% of people say they couldn't go a day without their smartphones. Until students are able to  __35__  the pull of social networking, texting, and endlessly surfing the web, they may continue to struggle in their classes.", "matchIntro": "A Pioneering Woman of Science Re-Emerges after 300 Years", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Maria Sibylla Merian, like many European women of the 17th century, stayed busy managing a household and rearing children. But on top of that, Merian, a German-born woman who lived in the Netherlands, also managed a successful career as an artist, botanist, naturalist and entomologist(昆虫学家)."}, {"label": "B", "text": "\"She was a scientist on the level with a lot of people we spend a lot of time talking about,\" said Kay Etheridge, a biologist at Gettysburg College in Pennsylvania who has been studying the scientific history of Merian's work. \"She didn't do as much to change biology as Charles Darwin, but she was significant.\""}, {"label": "C", "text": "At a time when natural history was a valuable tool for discovery, Merian discovered facts about plants and insects that were not previously known. Her observations helped dismiss the popular belief that insects spontaneously emerged from mud. The knowledge she collected over decades didn't just satisfy those curious about nature, but also provided valuable insights into medicine and science. She was the first to bring together insects and their habitats, including food they ate, into a single ecological composition."}, {"label": "D", "text": "After years of pleasing a fascinated audience across Europe with books of detailed descriptions and life-size paintings of familiar insects, in 1699 she sailed with her daughter nearly 5,000 miles from the Netherlands to South America to study insects in the jungles of what is now known as Suriname. She was 52 years old. The result was her masterpiece, Metamorphosis Insectorum Surinamensium."}, {"label": "E", "text": "In her work, she revealed a side of nature so exotic, dramatic and valuable to Europeans of the time that she received much acclaim. But a century later, her findings came under scientific criticism. Shoddy(粗糙的) reproductions of her work along with setbacks to women's roles in 18th- and 19th-century Europe resulted in her efforts being largely forgotten. \"It was kind of stunning when she sort of dropped off into oblivion(遗忘),\" said Dr. Etheridge. \"Victorians started putting women in a box, and they're still trying to crawl out of it.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "Today, the pioneering woman of the sciences has re-emerged. In recent years, feminists, historians and artists have all praised Merian's tenacity(坚韧), talent and inspirational artistic compositions. And now biologists like Dr. Etheridge are digging into the scientific texts that accompanied her art. Three hundred years after her death, Merian will be celebrated at an international symposium in Amsterdam this June."}, {"label": "G", "text": "And last month, Metamorphosis Insectorum Surinamensium was republished. It contains 60 plates(插图) and original descriptions, along with stories about Merian's life and updated scientific descriptions. Before writing Metamorphosis, Merian spent decades documenting European plants and insects that she published in a series of books. She began in her 20s, making textless, decorative paintings of flowers with insects. \"Then she got really serious,\" Dr. Etheridge said. Merian started raising insects at home, mostly butterflies and caterpillars. \"She would sit up all night until they came out of the pupa(蛹) so she could draw them,\" she said."}, {"label": "H", "text": "The results of her decades' worth of careful observations were detailed paintings and descriptions of European insects, followed by unconventional visuals and stories of insects and animals from a land that most at the time could only imagine. It's possible Merian used a magnifying glass to capture the detail of the split tongues of sphinx moths(斯芬克斯飞蛾) depicted in the painting. She wrote that the two tongues combine to form one tube for drinking nectar(花蜜). Some criticized this detail later, saying there was just one tongue, but Merian wasn't wrong. She may have observed the adult moth just as it emerged from its pupa. For a brief moment during that stage of its life cycle, the tongue consists of two tiny half-tubes before merging into one."}, {"label": "I", "text": "It may not have been ladylike to depict a giant spider devouring a hummingbird, but when Merian did it at the turn of the 18th century, surprisingly, nobody objected. Dr. Etheridge called it revolutionary. The image, which also contained novel descriptions of ants, fascinated a European audience that was more concerned with the exotic story unfolding before them than the gender of the person who painted it."}, {"label": "J", "text": "\"All of these things shook up their nice, neat little view,\" Dr. Etheridge said. But later, people of the Victorian era thought differently. Her work had been reproduced, sometimes incorrectly. A few observations were deemed impossible. \"She'd been called a silly woman for saying that a spider could eat a bird,\" Dr. Etheridge said. But Henry Walter Bates, a friend of Charles Darwin, observed it and put it in a book in 1863, proving Merian was correct."}, {"label": "K", "text": "In the same plate, Merian depicted and described leaf-cutter ants for the first time. \"In America there are large ants which can eat whole trees bare as a broom handle in a single night,\" she wrote in the description. Merian noted how the ants took the leaves below ground to their young. And she wouldn't have known this at the time, but the ants use the leaves to farm fungi(菌类) underground to feed their developing babies."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Merian was correct about the giant bird-eating spiders, ants building bridges with their bodies and other details. But in the same drawing, she incorrectly lumped together army and leaf-cutter ants. And instead of showing just the typical pair of eggs in a hummingbird nest, she painted four. She made other mistakes in Metamorphosis Insectorum Surinamensium as well: not every caterpillar and butterfly matched."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Perhaps one explanation for her mistakes is that she cut short her Suriname trip after getting sick, and completed the book at home in Amsterdam. And errors are common among some of history's most-celebrated scientific minds, too. \"These errors no more invalidate Ms. Merian's work than do well-known misconceptions published by Charles Darwin or Isaac Newton,\" Dr. Etheridge wrote in a paper that argued that too many have wrongly focused on the mistakes of her work."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Merian's paintings inspired artists and ecologists. In an 1801 drawing from his book, General Zoology Amphibia, George Shaw, an English botanist and zoologist, credited Merian for describing a frog in the account of her South American expedition, and named the young tree frog after her in his portrayal of it. It wouldn't be fair to give Merian all the credit. She received assistance naming plants, making sketches and referencing the work of others. Her daughters helped her color her drawings."}, {"label": "O", "text": "Merian also made note of the help she received from the natives of Suriname, as well as slaves or servants that assisted her. In some instances she wrote moving passages that included her helpers in descriptions. As she wrote in her description of the peacock flower, \"The Indians, who are not treated well by their Dutch masters, use the seeds to abort their children, so that they will not become slaves like themselves. The black slaves from Guinea and Angola have demanded to be well treated, threatening to refuse to have children. In fact, they sometimes take their own lives because they are treated so badly, and because they believe they will be born again, free and living in their own land. They told me this themselves.\""}, {"label": "P", "text": "Londa Schiebinger, a professor of the history of science at Stanford University, called this passage rather astonishing. It's particularly striking centuries later when these issues are still prominent in public discussions about social justice and women's rights. \"She was ahead of her time,\" Dr. Etheridge said."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "While human achievements in mathematics continue to reach new levels of complexity, many of us who aren't mathematicians at heart (or engineers by trade) may struggle to remember the last time we used calculus(微积分).\nIt's a fact not lost on American educators, who amid rising math failure rates are debating how math can better meet the real-life needs of students. Should we change the way math is taught in schools, or eliminate some courses entirely?\nAndrew Hacker, Queens College political science professor, thinks that advanced algebra and other higher-level math should be cut from curricula in favor of courses with more routine usefulness, like statistics.\n\"We hear on all sides that we're not teaching enough mathematics, and the Chinese are running rings around us,\" Hacker says. \"I'm suggesting we're teaching too much mathematics to too many people…not everybody has to know calculus. If you're going to become an aeronautical(航空的) engineer, fine. But most of us aren't.\"\nInstead, Hacker is pushing for more courses like the one he teaches at Queens College: Numeracy 101. There, his students of \"citizen statistics\" learn to analyze public information like the federal budget and corporate reports. Such courses, Hacker argues, are a remedy for the numerical illiteracy of adults who have completed high-level math like algebra but are unable to calculate the price of, say, a carpet by area.\nHacker's argument has met with opposition from other math educators who say what's needed is to help students develop a better relationship with math earlier, rather than teaching them less math altogether.\nMaria Droujkova is a founder of Natural Math, and has taught basic calculus concepts to 5-year-olds. For Droujkova, high-level math is important, and what it could use in American classrooms is an injection of childlike wonder.\n\"Make mathematics more available,\" Droujkova says. \"Redesign it so it's more accessible to more kinds of people: young children, adults who worry about it, adults who may have had bad experiences.\"\nPamela Harris, a lecturer at the University of Texas at Austin, has a similar perspective. Harris says that American education is suffering from an epidemic of \"fake math\"—an emphasis on rote memorization(死记硬背) of formulas and steps, rather than an understanding of how math can influence the ways we see the world.\nAndrew Hacker, for the record, remains skeptical.\n\"I'm going to leave it to those who are in mathematics to work out the ways to make their subject interesting and exciting so students want to take it,\" Hacker says. \"All that I ask is that alternatives be offered instead of putting all of us on the road to calculus.\"", "47": "While human achievements in mathematics continue to reach new levels of complexity, many of us who aren't mathematicians at heart (or engineers by trade) may struggle to remember the last time we used calculus(微积分).\nIt's a fact not lost on American educators, who amid rising math failure rates are debating how math can better meet the real-life needs of students. Should we change the way math is taught in schools, or eliminate some courses entirely?\nAndrew Hacker, Queens College political science professor, thinks that advanced algebra and other higher-level math should be cut from curricula in favor of courses with more routine usefulness, like statistics.\n\"We hear on all sides that we're not teaching enough mathematics, and the Chinese are running rings around us,\" Hacker says. \"I'm suggesting we're teaching too much mathematics to too many people…not everybody has to know calculus. If you're going to become an aeronautical(航空的) engineer, fine. But most of us aren't.\"\nInstead, Hacker is pushing for more courses like the one he teaches at Queens College: Numeracy 101. There, his students of \"citizen statistics\" learn to analyze public information like the federal budget and corporate reports. Such courses, Hacker argues, are a remedy for the numerical illiteracy of adults who have completed high-level math like algebra but are unable to calculate the price of, say, a carpet by area.\nHacker's argument has met with opposition from other math educators who say what's needed is to help students develop a better relationship with math earlier, rather than teaching them less math altogether.\nMaria Droujkova is a founder of Natural Math, and has taught basic calculus concepts to 5-year-olds. For Droujkova, high-level math is important, and what it could use in American classrooms is an injection of childlike wonder.\n\"Make mathematics more available,\" Droujkova says. \"Redesign it so it's more accessible to more kinds of people: young children, adults who worry about it, adults who may have had bad experiences.\"\nPamela Harris, a lecturer at the University of Texas at Austin, has a similar perspective. Harris says that American education is suffering from an epidemic of \"fake math\"—an emphasis on rote memorization(死记硬背) of formulas and steps, rather than an understanding of how math can influence the ways we see the world.\nAndrew Hacker, for the record, remains skeptical.\n\"I'm going to leave it to those who are in mathematics to work out the ways to make their subject interesting and exciting so students want to take it,\" Hacker says. \"All that I ask is that alternatives be offered instead of putting all of us on the road to calculus.\"", "48": "While human achievements in mathematics continue to reach new levels of complexity, many of us who aren't mathematicians at heart (or engineers by trade) may struggle to remember the last time we used calculus(微积分).\nIt's a fact not lost on American educators, who amid rising math failure rates are debating how math can better meet the real-life needs of students. Should we change the way math is taught in schools, or eliminate some courses entirely?\nAndrew Hacker, Queens College political science professor, thinks that advanced algebra and other higher-level math should be cut from curricula in favor of courses with more routine usefulness, like statistics.\n\"We hear on all sides that we're not teaching enough mathematics, and the Chinese are running rings around us,\" Hacker says. \"I'm suggesting we're teaching too much mathematics to too many people…not everybody has to know calculus. If you're going to become an aeronautical(航空的) engineer, fine. But most of us aren't.\"\nInstead, Hacker is pushing for more courses like the one he teaches at Queens College: Numeracy 101. There, his students of \"citizen statistics\" learn to analyze public information like the federal budget and corporate reports. Such courses, Hacker argues, are a remedy for the numerical illiteracy of adults who have completed high-level math like algebra but are unable to calculate the price of, say, a carpet by area.\nHacker's argument has met with opposition from other math educators who say what's needed is to help students develop a better relationship with math earlier, rather than teaching them less math altogether.\nMaria Droujkova is a founder of Natural Math, and has taught basic calculus concepts to 5-year-olds. For Droujkova, high-level math is important, and what it could use in American classrooms is an injection of childlike wonder.\n\"Make mathematics more available,\" Droujkova says. \"Redesign it so it's more accessible to more kinds of people: young children, adults who worry about it, adults who may have had bad experiences.\"\nPamela Harris, a lecturer at the University of Texas at Austin, has a similar perspective. Harris says that American education is suffering from an epidemic of \"fake math\"—an emphasis on rote memorization(死记硬背) of formulas and steps, rather than an understanding of how math can influence the ways we see the world.\nAndrew Hacker, for the record, remains skeptical.\n\"I'm going to leave it to those who are in mathematics to work out the ways to make their subject interesting and exciting so students want to take it,\" Hacker says. \"All that I ask is that alternatives be offered instead of putting all of us on the road to calculus.\"", "49": "While human achievements in mathematics continue to reach new levels of complexity, many of us who aren't mathematicians at heart (or engineers by trade) may struggle to remember the last time we used calculus(微积分).\nIt's a fact not lost on American educators, who amid rising math failure rates are debating how math can better meet the real-life needs of students. Should we change the way math is taught in schools, or eliminate some courses entirely?\nAndrew Hacker, Queens College political science professor, thinks that advanced algebra and other higher-level math should be cut from curricula in favor of courses with more routine usefulness, like statistics.\n\"We hear on all sides that we're not teaching enough mathematics, and the Chinese are running rings around us,\" Hacker says. \"I'm suggesting we're teaching too much mathematics to too many people…not everybody has to know calculus. If you're going to become an aeronautical(航空的) engineer, fine. But most of us aren't.\"\nInstead, Hacker is pushing for more courses like the one he teaches at Queens College: Numeracy 101. There, his students of \"citizen statistics\" learn to analyze public information like the federal budget and corporate reports. Such courses, Hacker argues, are a remedy for the numerical illiteracy of adults who have completed high-level math like algebra but are unable to calculate the price of, say, a carpet by area.\nHacker's argument has met with opposition from other math educators who say what's needed is to help students develop a better relationship with math earlier, rather than teaching them less math altogether.\nMaria Droujkova is a founder of Natural Math, and has taught basic calculus concepts to 5-year-olds. For Droujkova, high-level math is important, and what it could use in American classrooms is an injection of childlike wonder.\n\"Make mathematics more available,\" Droujkova says. \"Redesign it so it's more accessible to more kinds of people: young children, adults who worry about it, adults who may have had bad experiences.\"\nPamela Harris, a lecturer at the University of Texas at Austin, has a similar perspective. Harris says that American education is suffering from an epidemic of \"fake math\"—an emphasis on rote memorization(死记硬背) of formulas and steps, rather than an understanding of how math can influence the ways we see the world.\nAndrew Hacker, for the record, remains skeptical.\n\"I'm going to leave it to those who are in mathematics to work out the ways to make their subject interesting and exciting so students want to take it,\" Hacker says. \"All that I ask is that alternatives be offered instead of putting all of us on the road to calculus.\"", "50": "While human achievements in mathematics continue to reach new levels of complexity, many of us who aren't mathematicians at heart (or engineers by trade) may struggle to remember the last time we used calculus(微积分).\nIt's a fact not lost on American educators, who amid rising math failure rates are debating how math can better meet the real-life needs of students. Should we change the way math is taught in schools, or eliminate some courses entirely?\nAndrew Hacker, Queens College political science professor, thinks that advanced algebra and other higher-level math should be cut from curricula in favor of courses with more routine usefulness, like statistics.\n\"We hear on all sides that we're not teaching enough mathematics, and the Chinese are running rings around us,\" Hacker says. \"I'm suggesting we're teaching too much mathematics to too many people…not everybody has to know calculus. If you're going to become an aeronautical(航空的) engineer, fine. But most of us aren't.\"\nInstead, Hacker is pushing for more courses like the one he teaches at Queens College: Numeracy 101. There, his students of \"citizen statistics\" learn to analyze public information like the federal budget and corporate reports. Such courses, Hacker argues, are a remedy for the numerical illiteracy of adults who have completed high-level math like algebra but are unable to calculate the price of, say, a carpet by area.\nHacker's argument has met with opposition from other math educators who say what's needed is to help students develop a better relationship with math earlier, rather than teaching them less math altogether.\nMaria Droujkova is a founder of Natural Math, and has taught basic calculus concepts to 5-year-olds. For Droujkova, high-level math is important, and what it could use in American classrooms is an injection of childlike wonder.\n\"Make mathematics more available,\" Droujkova says. \"Redesign it so it's more accessible to more kinds of people: young children, adults who worry about it, adults who may have had bad experiences.\"\nPamela Harris, a lecturer at the University of Texas at Austin, has a similar perspective. Harris says that American education is suffering from an epidemic of \"fake math\"—an emphasis on rote memorization(死记硬背) of formulas and steps, rather than an understanding of how math can influence the ways we see the world.\nAndrew Hacker, for the record, remains skeptical.\n\"I'm going to leave it to those who are in mathematics to work out the ways to make their subject interesting and exciting so students want to take it,\" Hacker says. \"All that I ask is that alternatives be offered instead of putting all of us on the road to calculus.\"", "51": "For years, the U.S. has experienced a shortage of registered nurses. The Bureau of Labor Statistics projects that while the number of nurses will increase by 19 percent by 2022, demand will grow faster than supply, and that there will be over one million unfilled nursing jobs by then.\nSo what's the solution? Robots.\nJapan is ahead of the curve when it comes to this trend. Toyohashi University of Technology has developed Terapio, a robotic medical cart that can make hospital rounds, deliver medications and other items, and retrieve records. It follows a specific individual, such as a doctor or nurse, who can use it to record and access patient data. This type of robot will likely be one of the first to be implemented in hospitals because it has fairly minimal patient contact.\nRobots capable of social engagement help with loneliness as well as cognitive functioning, but the robot itself doesn't have to engage directly—it can serve as an intermediary for human communication. Telepresence robots such as MantaroBot, Vgo, and Giraff can be controlled through a computer, smartphone, or tablet, allowing family members or doctors to remotely monitor patients or Skype them, often via a screen where the robot's 'face' would be. If you can't get to the nursing home to visit grandma, you can use a telepresence robot to hang out with her. A 2016 study found that users had a \"consistently positive attitude\" about the Giraff robot's ability to enhance communication and decrease feelings of loneliness.\nA robot's appearance affects its ability to successfully interact with humans, which is why the RIKEN-TRI Collaboration Center for Human-Interactive Robot Research decided to develop a robotic nurse that looks like a huge teddy bear. RIBA (Robot for Interactive Body Assistance), also known as \"Robear,\" can help patients into and out of wheelchairs and beds with its strong arms.\nOn the less cute and more scary side there is Actroid F, which is so human-like that some patients may not know the difference. This conversational robot companion has cameras in its eyes, which allow it to track patients and use appropriate facial expressions and body language in its interactions. During a month-long hospital trial, researchers asked 70 patients how they felt being around the robot and \"only three or four said they didn't like having it around.\"\nIt's important to note that robotic nurses don't decide courses of treatment or make diagnoses (though robot doctors and surgeons may not be far off). Instead, they perform routine and laborious tasks, freeing nurses up to attend to patients with immediate needs. This is one industry where it seems the integration of robots will lead to collaboration, not replacement.", "52": "For years, the U.S. has experienced a shortage of registered nurses. The Bureau of Labor Statistics projects that while the number of nurses will increase by 19 percent by 2022, demand will grow faster than supply, and that there will be over one million unfilled nursing jobs by then.\nSo what's the solution? Robots.\nJapan is ahead of the curve when it comes to this trend. Toyohashi University of Technology has developed Terapio, a robotic medical cart that can make hospital rounds, deliver medications and other items, and retrieve records. It follows a specific individual, such as a doctor or nurse, who can use it to record and access patient data. This type of robot will likely be one of the first to be implemented in hospitals because it has fairly minimal patient contact.\nRobots capable of social engagement help with loneliness as well as cognitive functioning, but the robot itself doesn't have to engage directly—it can serve as an intermediary for human communication. Telepresence robots such as MantaroBot, Vgo, and Giraff can be controlled through a computer, smartphone, or tablet, allowing family members or doctors to remotely monitor patients or Skype them, often via a screen where the robot's 'face' would be. If you can't get to the nursing home to visit grandma, you can use a telepresence robot to hang out with her. A 2016 study found that users had a \"consistently positive attitude\" about the Giraff robot's ability to enhance communication and decrease feelings of loneliness.\nA robot's appearance affects its ability to successfully interact with humans, which is why the RIKEN-TRI Collaboration Center for Human-Interactive Robot Research decided to develop a robotic nurse that looks like a huge teddy bear. RIBA (Robot for Interactive Body Assistance), also known as \"Robear,\" can help patients into and out of wheelchairs and beds with its strong arms.\nOn the less cute and more scary side there is Actroid F, which is so human-like that some patients may not know the difference. This conversational robot companion has cameras in its eyes, which allow it to track patients and use appropriate facial expressions and body language in its interactions. During a month-long hospital trial, researchers asked 70 patients how they felt being around the robot and \"only three or four said they didn't like having it around.\"\nIt's important to note that robotic nurses don't decide courses of treatment or make diagnoses (though robot doctors and surgeons may not be far off). Instead, they perform routine and laborious tasks, freeing nurses up to attend to patients with immediate needs. This is one industry where it seems the integration of robots will lead to collaboration, not replacement.", "53": "For years, the U.S. has experienced a shortage of registered nurses. The Bureau of Labor Statistics projects that while the number of nurses will increase by 19 percent by 2022, demand will grow faster than supply, and that there will be over one million unfilled nursing jobs by then.\nSo what's the solution? Robots.\nJapan is ahead of the curve when it comes to this trend. Toyohashi University of Technology has developed Terapio, a robotic medical cart that can make hospital rounds, deliver medications and other items, and retrieve records. It follows a specific individual, such as a doctor or nurse, who can use it to record and access patient data. This type of robot will likely be one of the first to be implemented in hospitals because it has fairly minimal patient contact.\nRobots capable of social engagement help with loneliness as well as cognitive functioning, but the robot itself doesn't have to engage directly—it can serve as an intermediary for human communication. Telepresence robots such as MantaroBot, Vgo, and Giraff can be controlled through a computer, smartphone, or tablet, allowing family members or doctors to remotely monitor patients or Skype them, often via a screen where the robot's 'face' would be. If you can't get to the nursing home to visit grandma, you can use a telepresence robot to hang out with her. A 2016 study found that users had a \"consistently positive attitude\" about the Giraff robot's ability to enhance communication and decrease feelings of loneliness.\nA robot's appearance affects its ability to successfully interact with humans, which is why the RIKEN-TRI Collaboration Center for Human-Interactive Robot Research decided to develop a robotic nurse that looks like a huge teddy bear. RIBA (Robot for Interactive Body Assistance), also known as \"Robear,\" can help patients into and out of wheelchairs and beds with its strong arms.\nOn the less cute and more scary side there is Actroid F, which is so human-like that some patients may not know the difference. This conversational robot companion has cameras in its eyes, which allow it to track patients and use appropriate facial expressions and body language in its interactions. During a month-long hospital trial, researchers asked 70 patients how they felt being around the robot and \"only three or four said they didn't like having it around.\"\nIt's important to note that robotic nurses don't decide courses of treatment or make diagnoses (though robot doctors and surgeons may not be far off). Instead, they perform routine and laborious tasks, freeing nurses up to attend to patients with immediate needs. This is one industry where it seems the integration of robots will lead to collaboration, not replacement.", "54": "For years, the U.S. has experienced a shortage of registered nurses. The Bureau of Labor Statistics projects that while the number of nurses will increase by 19 percent by 2022, demand will grow faster than supply, and that there will be over one million unfilled nursing jobs by then.\nSo what's the solution? Robots.\nJapan is ahead of the curve when it comes to this trend. Toyohashi University of Technology has developed Terapio, a robotic medical cart that can make hospital rounds, deliver medications and other items, and retrieve records. It follows a specific individual, such as a doctor or nurse, who can use it to record and access patient data. This type of robot will likely be one of the first to be implemented in hospitals because it has fairly minimal patient contact.\nRobots capable of social engagement help with loneliness as well as cognitive functioning, but the robot itself doesn't have to engage directly—it can serve as an intermediary for human communication. Telepresence robots such as MantaroBot, Vgo, and Giraff can be controlled through a computer, smartphone, or tablet, allowing family members or doctors to remotely monitor patients or Skype them, often via a screen where the robot's 'face' would be. If you can't get to the nursing home to visit grandma, you can use a telepresence robot to hang out with her. A 2016 study found that users had a \"consistently positive attitude\" about the Giraff robot's ability to enhance communication and decrease feelings of loneliness.\nA robot's appearance affects its ability to successfully interact with humans, which is why the RIKEN-TRI Collaboration Center for Human-Interactive Robot Research decided to develop a robotic nurse that looks like a huge teddy bear. RIBA (Robot for Interactive Body Assistance), also known as \"Robear,\" can help patients into and out of wheelchairs and beds with its strong arms.\nOn the less cute and more scary side there is Actroid F, which is so human-like that some patients may not know the difference. This conversational robot companion has cameras in its eyes, which allow it to track patients and use appropriate facial expressions and body language in its interactions. During a month-long hospital trial, researchers asked 70 patients how they felt being around the robot and \"only three or four said they didn't like having it around.\"\nIt's important to note that robotic nurses don't decide courses of treatment or make diagnoses (though robot doctors and surgeons may not be far off). Instead, they perform routine and laborious tasks, freeing nurses up to attend to patients with immediate needs. This is one industry where it seems the integration of robots will lead to collaboration, not replacement.", "55": "For years, the U.S. has experienced a shortage of registered nurses. The Bureau of Labor Statistics projects that while the number of nurses will increase by 19 percent by 2022, demand will grow faster than supply, and that there will be over one million unfilled nursing jobs by then.\nSo what's the solution? Robots.\nJapan is ahead of the curve when it comes to this trend. Toyohashi University of Technology has developed Terapio, a robotic medical cart that can make hospital rounds, deliver medications and other items, and retrieve records. It follows a specific individual, such as a doctor or nurse, who can use it to record and access patient data. This type of robot will likely be one of the first to be implemented in hospitals because it has fairly minimal patient contact.\nRobots capable of social engagement help with loneliness as well as cognitive functioning, but the robot itself doesn't have to engage directly—it can serve as an intermediary for human communication. Telepresence robots such as MantaroBot, Vgo, and Giraff can be controlled through a computer, smartphone, or tablet, allowing family members or doctors to remotely monitor patients or Skype them, often via a screen where the robot's 'face' would be. If you can't get to the nursing home to visit grandma, you can use a telepresence robot to hang out with her. A 2016 study found that users had a \"consistently positive attitude\" about the Giraff robot's ability to enhance communication and decrease feelings of loneliness.\nA robot's appearance affects its ability to successfully interact with humans, which is why the RIKEN-TRI Collaboration Center for Human-Interactive Robot Research decided to develop a robotic nurse that looks like a huge teddy bear. RIBA (Robot for Interactive Body Assistance), also known as \"Robear,\" can help patients into and out of wheelchairs and beds with its strong arms.\nOn the less cute and more scary side there is Actroid F, which is so human-like that some patients may not know the difference. This conversational robot companion has cameras in its eyes, which allow it to track patients and use appropriate facial expressions and body language in its interactions. During a month-long hospital trial, researchers asked 70 patients how they felt being around the robot and \"only three or four said they didn't like having it around.\"\nIt's important to note that robotic nurses don't decide courses of treatment or make diagnoses (though robot doctors and surgeons may not be far off). Instead, they perform routine and laborious tasks, freeing nurses up to attend to patients with immediate needs. This is one industry where it seems the integration of robots will lead to collaboration, not replacement."}, "questions": [{"id": "2018-12-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What does the man ask the woman to do?", "options": ["A) Stop worrying about him.", "B) Keep away from the statue.", "C) Take a picture of him.", "D) Put on a smile for the photo."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Take a picture of him.\n【定位】M: Do you mind taking my photo with the statue over there? I think it will make a great shot.\n男：你介意帮我跟那边的雕像拍张照吗？我觉得会拍出很棒的一张。\n【信号】礼貌请求句 Do you mind。答案信号是请求句式 Do you mind taking my photo——Do\nyou mind doing 是典型的「麻烦你做某事」，男方开口要对方做的动作就藏在 mind 后面\n的 taking my photo 里。\n【替换】taking my photo ↔ Take a picture of him\n同义替换 photo=picture\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n女方回答里有 no worries（没问题），选项 Stop worrying about him. 盗用了 worry 这\n个词，但「别担心他」根本不是男方提出的请求，属听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：no worries\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音确实提到 with the statue over there，选项 Keep away from the statue. 借了\nstatue 这个词，但男方是想「和雕像合影」，绝非「远离雕像」，方向恰好相反。\n借⽤原⽂：with the statue over there\nD) 未提及\nPut on a smile for the photo. 是「拍照要笑一笑」的常识联想，但录音里男方只请求拍\n照、从未要求对方微笑，属凭生活经验脑补、原文未提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the man dream of?", "options": ["A) Gaining great fame on the Internet.", "B) Publishing a collection of his photos.", "C) Collecting the best photos in the world.", "D) Becoming a professional photographer."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Gaining great fame on the Internet.\n【定位】M: Well, don't laugh. My dream is to become an online celebrity of sorts.\n男：嗯，别笑啊。我的梦想是成为某种意义上的网络名人。\n【信号】表愿望句 My dream is。答案信号是直接的表愿望句 My dream is to become an online\ncelebrity——My dream is 后面紧跟的就是梦想本体，是最显眼的落点信号。\n【替换】an online celebrity ↔ Gaining great fame on the Internet\n概念改写 celebrity=fame\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音反复提到 photos，选项 Publishing a collection of his photos. 借了 photos 并升级\n成「出一本影集」，但男方从没说要出版，属把原词嫁接到未提及的目标上。\n借⽤原⽂：posting some holiday photos\nC) 原词陷阱\nCollecting the best photos in the world. 同样借 photos 一词，编成「收集全世界最好的\n照片」，录音里男方是想自己出名、不是收藏照片，方向被偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：posting some holiday photos\nD) 过度推断\nBecoming a professional photographer. 是「爱拍照→当职业摄影师」的顺理成章联想，\n但男方明确说梦想是当网络名人、不是职业摄影师，属过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：My dream is to become an online celebrity of sorts", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What has the man been busy doing recently?", "options": ["A) Surfing various websites and collecting photos.", "B) Editing his pictures and posting them online.", "C) Following similar accounts to compare notes.", "D) Studying the pictures in popular social media."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Editing his pictures and posting them online.\n【定位】M: Surprisingly a lot more than I did as a hobby. Recently, I've been spending a lot more time\nediting photos, posting online and clearing storage on my phone.\n男：出乎意料地比当业余爱好时多得多。最近我花了多得多的时间修图、发到网上、清理手\n机存储空间。\n【信号】时间状语 Recently 引出近期动作。答案信号是时间词 Recently, I've been spending a lot\nmore time editing photos, posting online——Recently 加现在完成进行时，正面回应\n「最近在忙什么」，其后列举的动作就是答案。\n【替换】editing photos ↔ Editing his pictures\n近义 photos=pictures\nposting online ↔ posting them online\n几乎原句对应\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nSurfing various websites and collecting photos. 里 collecting photos 像在呼应拍照话\n题，但「最近」男方做的是修图+发帖，不是浏览网站收集照片；这是把别处或想象的动作\n安到「最近」上。\n借⽤原⽂：Recently, I've been spending a lot more time editing photos, posting online\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音第9段确有 similar accounts to follow，选项 Following similar accounts to\ncompare notes. 盗了 similar accounts，但那是男方上周末研究热门话题时做的事、且并\n无 compare notes 之意，与「最近在忙」的修图发帖不是同一动作。\n借⽤原⽂：similar accounts to follow\nD) 偷换概念\n录音第9段提到 researching what topics are popular，选项 Studying the pictures in\npopular social media. 借了 popular 与 social media，但男方研究的是「热门话题」而非\n「研究社媒上的图片」，动作对象被偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：researching what topics are popular", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the woman say about her photos?", "options": ["A) They are far from satisfactory.", "B) They are mostly taken by her mom.", "C) They make an impressive album.", "D) They record her fond memories."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） They are far from satisfactory.\n【定位】W: Now that I think about it, there's always something wrong with my photos as it is—half\nsmiles, closed eyes, messy hair.\n女：这么一想，我的照片总是有点毛病——笑得不自然、闭着眼、头发乱糟糟。\n【信号】含蓄否定 something wrong。答案信号是负面评价词 there's always something wrong\nwith my photos——something wrong 直接给出「总有毛病」的差评，后面 half\nsmiles、closed eyes、messy hair 是具体吐槽，态度是明确的不满意。\n【替换】there's always something wrong with my photos ↔ They are far from satisfactory\n差评互译 wrong=far from satisfactory\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\n录音末尾确有 the only person interested in my photos is my mom，选项 They are\nmostly taken by her mom. 盗用 mom 一词，但原文是「只有妈妈对我的照片感兴趣」，\n并非「照片大多是妈妈拍的」，把「感兴趣」偷换成「拍摄者」。\n借⽤原⽂：the only person interested in my photos is my mom\nC) 与原文相反\nThey make an impressive album. 是正面评价，而女方明说照片总有毛病，态度恰好相\n反；impressive 一词录音里出现过，但那是女方夸男方努力 That is impressive，与她自己\n的照片无关。\n借⽤原⽂：That is impressive\nD) 未提及\nThey record her fond memories.（记录美好回忆）是对照片的常见正面联想，但女方通篇\n在吐槽照片毛病，从未说它们承载美好回忆，属凭印象脑补、原文未提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the man say is Physics Today?", "options": ["A) A journal reporting the latest progress in physics.", "B) An introductory course of modern physics.", "C) An occasion for physicists to exchange ideas.", "D) A series of interviews with outstanding physicists."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） A series of interviews with outstanding physicists.\n【定位】Here we interview some of the greatest minds in physics as they help us to understand some\nof the most complicated theories.\n在这里我们采访物理学界一些最杰出的大脑，请他们帮助我们理解一些最复杂的理论。\n【信号】开场白的定性句。信号是节目最开头的自我介绍 Here we interview some of the greatest\nminds in physics——主持人 welcome 之后第一句就给节目下定义，'是什么'的答案几乎\n总落在开场白这种介绍句里。\n【替换】we interview some of the greatest minds in physics ↔\nA series of interviews with outstanding physicists.\n同义改写：采访杰出物理学家\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA journal reporting the latest progress in physics. 借了 physics 一词，但把节目说成'期\n刊'，而录音是口头 interview 节目，体裁不符。\n借⽤原⽂：welcome to Physics Today\nB) 未提及\nAn introductory course of modern physics. 把节目说成'现代物理入门课程'，录音通篇\n无'course/课程'之意，属凭空捏造。\nC) 偷换概念\nAn occasion for physicists to exchange ideas. 听着像，但录音是主持人单向 interview\n嘉宾，并非物理学家彼此 exchange ideas，把'采访'偷换成了'同行交流'。\n借⽤原⽂：Here we interview some of the greatest minds in physics", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What is the woman physicist's main research area?", "options": ["A) The future of the physical world.", "B) The origin of the universe.", "C) Sources of radiation.", "D) Particle theory."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） The origin of the universe.\n【定位】I spend most of my time studying the Big Bang theory and where our universe came from.\n我大部分时间都在研究大爆炸理论，以及我们的宇宙从何而来。\n【信号】most of my time 标主次。信号词 I spend most of my time studying 明确限定'花最多\n时间'，把主攻方向与顺带话题区分开，是定位'main research'的关键标志。\n【替换】where our universe came from ↔ The origin of the universe.\n同义改写：宇宙从何而来=起源\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThe future of the physical world. 与正解方向相反，录音问的是 where our universe\ncame from（来处/起源），不是 future（去向/未来）。\nC) 原词陷阱\nSources of radiation. 里 radiation 后文确被念到 a super-hot soup of radiation，但那只\n是描述大爆炸瞬间的状态，并非她的'主要研究领域'，是听到熟词就想选的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：The whole universe was a super-hot soup of radiation\nD) 原词陷阱\nParticle theory. 与录音 filled with these particles 形近，但'particle theory'是另一个理\n论名，录音并未说这是她的主研究方向，属原词诱导。\n借⽤原⽂：filled with these particles", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What is the woman interested in?", "options": ["A) How matter collides with anti-matter.", "B) Whether the universe will turn barren.", "C) Why there exists anti-matter.", "D) Why there is a universe at all."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Why there is a universe at all.\n【定位】Well, I'm very interested in why the universe exists at all.\n嗯，我非常感兴趣的是：为什么宇宙竟然会存在。\n【信号】interested in 后的宾语。信号是兴趣标志短语 I'm very interested in why the universe\nexists at all——'interested in' 与题干 interested in 同形，其后从句即兴趣对象，直接\n锁定。\n【替换】why the universe exists at all ↔ Why there is a universe at all.\n近乎逐词对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nHow matter collides with anti-matter. 借了后文 when they collide, they destroy each\nother 的 collide，但'相撞过程'是主持人解释的科普背景，不是她自陈的兴趣点。\n借⽤原⽂：when they collide, they destroy each other\nB) 原词陷阱\nWhether the universe will turn barren. 中 barren 来自后文 leaving the universe\ncompletely barren，但那是推演反物质对撞的后果，并非她'感兴趣'的问题，是熟词错位。\n借⽤原⽂：leaving the universe completely barren\nC) 偷换概念\nWhy there exists anti-matter. 与正解只差宾语（anti-matter vs universe），录音她说的\n是 the universe exists，把'宇宙'偷换成'反物质'，正中孪生陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：why the universe exists at all", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What seems to be the finding of the recent study?", "options": ["A) Matter and anti-matter are opposites of each other.", "B) Anti-matter allowed humans to come into existence.", "C) The universe formed due to a sufficient amount of matter.", "D) Anti-matter exists in very high-temperature environments."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The universe formed due to a sufficient amount of matter.\n【定位】But a recent study seems to point to the fact that when matter and anti-matter were first\ncreated, there were slightly more particles of matter, which allowed the universe we all live in\nto form?\n但最近一项研究似乎表明：当物质和反物质最初被创造出来时，物质的粒子稍微多一些，正\n是这一点让我们所生活的宇宙得以形成。\n【信号】study + point to the fact。信号是 a recent study seems to point to the fact that\n——'recent study'与题干 recent study 对应、'point to the fact'引出研究结论，结论句随\n即给出'物质稍多'这一发现。\n【替换】there were slightly more particles of matter, which allowed the universe we all live in to form ↔\nThe universe formed due to a sufficient amount of matter.\n因果同义：物质够多→宇宙形成\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nMatter and anti-matter are opposites of each other. 直接抄自 matter and anti-matter\nare basically opposites of each other，但那是第 5 段对二者关系的解释，不是'最新研究\n的发现'，原词原句却答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：matter and anti-matter are basically opposites of each other\nB) 与原文相反\nAnti-matter allowed humans to come into existence. 把'使人类出现'归功于 anti-\nmatter，但录音说是 matter 稍多才 left just enough matter to create stars, planets,\nand eventually us，功劳应归 matter，归因主体被反转。\n借⽤原⽂：left just enough matter to create stars, planets, and eventually us\nD) 原词陷阱\nAnti-matter exists in very high-temperature environments. 用 high-temperature 勾连\n录音 the universe was extremely hot，但录音只说大爆炸初期宇宙极热，从未说'反物质\n存在于高温环境'是研究发现，属熟词拼凑的无据陈述。\n借⽤原⽂：the universe was extremely hot and very small", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What happened to Michelle Myers one day?", "options": ["A) She found herself speaking a foreign language.", "B) She woke up speaking with a different accent.", "C) She found some symptoms of her illness gone.", "D) She woke up finding herself in another country."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） She woke up speaking with a different accent.\n【定位】Myers is an American woman who woke up one day speaking with a British accent, even\nthough she's lived in the United States all her life.\n迈尔斯是一名美国女性，某天醒来后说话带上了英国口音，尽管她一辈子都住在美国。\n【信号】题干同款词 woke up one day 直接对位。原文 woke up one day speaking with a\nBritish accent 与题干的 one day 用词高度对应，这就是承载答案的那一句；后半 even\nthough she's lived in the United States all her life 用让步强调\"明明是美国人却带英国\n腔\"，进一步坐实这是关键异常。\n【替换】speaking with a British accent ↔ speaking with a different accent\n具体口音→上位概括\nwoke up one day ↔ woke up\n时间状语原样保留\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nA 说她 speaking a foreign language，但原文是 speaking with a British accent——只\n是口音变了，英语还是英语，并没有改说外语；这是把\"口音\"偷换成\"语言\"。\n借⽤原⽂：speaking with a British accent\nC) 未提及\nC 说她 found some symptoms of her illness gone\"发现症状消失了\"，但原文只讲她醒来\n多了英国口音，从没说任何症状消失；属凭空添加。\nD) 过度推断\nD 说她 finding herself in another country，借的是原文 a British accent 里的\"英国\"联\n想；但原文明说她 lived in the United States all her life，人没去任何国家，只是口音像英\n国人。\n借⽤原⽂：a British accent", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the passage say about Foreign Accent Syndrome?", "options": ["A) It is usually caused by a stroke or brain injury.", "B) It has not yet found any effective treatment.", "C) It leaves the patient with a distorted memory.", "D) It often happens to people with speech defects."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It is usually caused by a stroke or brain injury.\n【定位】The condition is most often caused by a stroke or traumatic brain injury.\n这种病症最常见的成因是中风或外伤性脑损伤。\n【信号】因果信号 caused by 标出病因句。原文 most often caused by a stroke or traumatic\nbrain injury 用 caused by 这一明确的因果信号，直接交代病因，正是承载答案的那句；\nmost often 又点出\"最常见\"，与选项的\"usually\"对应。\n【替换】most often caused by a stroke or traumatic brain injury ↔\nusually caused by a stroke or brain injury\nmost often=usually，删修饰词\n【排除】B) 未提及\nB 说 has not yet found any effective treatment\"尚无有效疗法\"，原文通篇讲成因、表\n现、罕见度，从没谈治疗有没有效；属凭空添加。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说留下 a distorted memory\"扭曲的记忆\"，借的是原文 distort their pronunciation 的\ndistort；但原文 distort 修饰的是\"发音 pronunciation\"，不是\"记忆 memory\"，被偷换了\n对象。\n借⽤原⽂：distort their pronunciation\nD) 答非所问\nD 说它 happens to people with speech defects\"常发生在有言语缺陷的人身上\"，把言语\n问题当成\"易发人群\"的前提；但原文说的是患病后 may distort their pronunciation——言\n语异常是病的\"结果\"，原文也没说有言语缺陷的人更易患病。\n借⽤原⽂：may distort their pronunciation", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What accent did another American woman speak with after a head injury?", "options": ["A) British.", "B) Irish.", "C) Russian.", "D) Australian."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Russian.\n【定位】However, a different American woman reportedly spoke with a Russian accent in 2010 after\nshe fell down the stairs and hit her head.\n然而，另一名美国女性据报道在 2010 年摔下楼梯、撞到头之后，说话带上了俄罗斯口音。\n【信号】转折 However 引出\"另一个人\"的新例。原文 However, a different American woman 用\nHowever 把话题从主角迈尔斯转到\"另一名美国女性\"，正对应题干的 another American\nwoman；其后 after she fell down the stairs and hit her head 对应题干的 after a head\ninjury，限定语全部吻合，这就是答案句。\n【替换】spoke with a Russian accent ↔ Russian.\n口音名原词直取\nafter she fell down the stairs and hit her head ↔ Russian.\n限定语对齐保证抓对人\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nA British 是主角迈尔斯醒来后带的口音 a British accent，但题干问的是\"另一名女性\"，把\n主角的口音安到了别人头上，抓错了人。\n借⽤原⽂：a British accent\nB) 张冠李戴\nB Irish 来自原文说迈尔斯 Previously, Myers had woken up speaking in Irish and\nAustralian accents——那是主角先前的口音，依旧不是题干问的\"另一名女性\"，张冠李\n戴。\n借⽤原⽂：speaking in Irish and Australian accents\nD) 张冠李戴\nD Australian 同样出自迈尔斯先前的口音 Irish and Australian accents，是主角的旧情\n况，并非\"另一名头部受伤女性\"的口音。\n借⽤原⽂：Irish and Australian accents", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What has become so popular recently?", "options": ["A) Water sports.", "B) Racing in rivers.", "C) Stories about women swimmers.", "D) Books about swimming."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Books about swimming.\n【定位】And it goes some way towards explaining why books about swimming, in which people\ntackle icy lakes, race in rivers and overcome oceans while reflecting on their lives, have\nrecently become so popular.\n这也在一定程度上解释了，为什么那些关于游泳的书——书里人们挑战冰冷的湖泊、在河中\n竞速、征服大海，同时反思自己的人生——最近会变得如此受欢迎。\n【信号】popular 信号词直接点名。这句出现了和题干完全对上的信号词组 have recently become\nso popular，而被它修饰的主语正是 books about swimming，信号词正前方的那个名词\n就是答案对象。\n【替换】books about swimming ↔ Books about swimming.\n几乎原样对应\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音通篇没有把 water sports（水上运动）当成最近流行的对象来说，A 是把游泳这个话题\n擅自拔高成水上运动，属于原文没说的概括。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音里 race in rivers 确实念到了，但那是在描述书中内容（书里写人们在河中竞速），并\n不是说在河中竞速这件事最近流行。B 盗用了 race in rivers 这个原词来诱你听到就选。\n借⽤原⽂：race in rivers\nC) 答非所问\n录音第二段才提到女性 wild swimming 及女性先驱，但 C Stories about women\nswimmers. 不是第 12 题问的最近流行的东西；它把后文话题挪到了本题，属答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：swimming\" seems to be especially popular among women", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What did Jenny Landreth do recently?", "options": ["A) She succeeded in swimming across the English Channel.", "B) She published a guide to London's best swimming spots.", "C) She told her story of adventures to some young swimmers.", "D) She wrote a book about the history of swimwear in the UK."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） She published a guide to London's best swimming spots.\n【定位】Jenny Landreth recently published a guide to the best swimming spots in London.\n珍妮·兰德雷思最近出版了一本伦敦最佳游泳地点指南。\n【信号】人名后紧跟的动作句。信号是专有人名 Jenny Landreth recently published 出现后紧跟的\n那个动词 published，加上时间词 recently 与题干呼应，这句就是承载答案的动作句。\n【替换】published a guide to the best swimming spots in London ↔\npublished a guide to London's best swimming spots\n语序调整的同义改写\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n横渡英吉利海峡的是第四段的 Gertrude Ederle，不是 Jenny Landreth。A 盗用了录音里\ncross the English Channel 这件事，安到了 Jenny 头上。\n借⽤原⽂：The first woman to cross the English Channel was Gertrude Ederle\nC) 未提及\n录音从未说 Jenny 向年轻泳者讲述自己的冒险故事，C She told her story of adventures\nto some young swimmers. 是凭空捏造的动作。\nD) 偷换概念\n录音里 swimwear 出现在第三、四段讲历史的语境，并非说 Jenny 写了一本英国泳衣史。\nD 盗用了 swimwear、history 这类原词，把她出的指南偷换成泳衣史的书。\n借⽤原⽂：a history of female pioneers", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What do we learn about women in the Victorian era?", "options": ["A) They loved vacationing on the seashore.", "B) They had a unique notion of modesty.", "C) They were prohibited from swimming.", "D) They were fully dressed when swimming."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They were fully dressed when swimming.\n【定位】In 1892, The Gentlewoman's Book of Sport described a woman swimming in a heavy dress,\nboots, hat, gloves and carrying an umbrella.\n1892 年，《淑女运动手册》描述了一位女性穿着厚重的连衣裙、靴子、帽子、手套，还撑\n着伞游泳。\n【信号】细节罗列即具象描述。信号是录音对这位女性着装的一长串具体罗列 swimming in a heavy\ndress, boots, hat, gloves，这种把穿着一件件数出来的细节描述，正是在刻画当时女性是\n穿戴整齐地下水的，承载着答案。\n【替换】swimming in a heavy dress, boots, hat, gloves ↔ They were fully dressed when swimming.\n具体罗列概括为整体\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音提到 seashore（rolled down to the seashore）只是说沐浴机被推到海边，从未说她\n们热爱去海边度假。A 盗用了 seashore 相关语境，编出一个原文没有的喜好。\n借⽤原⽂：rolled down to the seashore\nB) 偷换概念\n录音确有 Notions of modesty restricted women，说端庄观念限制了女性，但并没有说\n她们有一种独特的端庄观念。B 盗用 modesty 一词，把限制她们的观念偷换成她们自己独\n有的观念，意思被拔高了。\n借⽤原⽂：Notions of modesty restricted women in the Victorian era\nC) 与原文相反\n录音明确说 but they still swam，即虽受限制但她们仍然游泳；C They were prohibited\nfrom swimming. 说她们被禁止游泳，正好相反。\n借⽤原⽂：but they still swam", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the passage say about Gertrude Ederle?", "options": ["A) She designed lots of appropriate swimwear for women.", "B) She once successfully competed against men in swimming.", "C) She was the first woman to swim across the English Channel.", "D) She was an advocate of women's right to swim in public pools."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） She was the first woman to swim across the English Channel.\n【定位】The first woman to cross the English Channel was Gertrude Ederle in 1926.\n第一个横渡英吉利海峡的女性是格特鲁德·埃德尔，时间是 1926 年。\n【信号】the first 唯一性表述。信号是 The first woman to cross the English Channel 里的 the\nfirst，这种表示第一 / 唯一的最高级式表述是听力的高频考点，它正前方的成就就是这位人\n物的标志事迹。\n【替换】The first woman to cross the English Channel was Gertrude Ederle ↔\nShe was the first woman to swim across the English Channel.\ncross 与 swim across 同义\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n设计合适泳衣是录音泛指女性群体做过的事（created appropriate swimwear），并非专\n说 Gertrude Ederle。A 盗用 appropriate swimwear 原词，把群体行为安到她一人头上。\n借⽤原⽂：created appropriate swimwear\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音说女性群体后来 even competed against men，但没说 Gertrude Ederle 战胜过男选\n手。B 盗用 competed against men 原词，又加了 successfully 制造她赢了男人的假象。\n借⽤原⽂：even competed against men\nD) 偷换概念\n录音说女性 won the right to swim in public pools，是讲群体争得权利，并非说\nGertrude Ederle 是公共泳池游泳权的倡导者。D 盗用 right to swim in public pools 原\n词，张冠李戴又拔高成倡导者。\n借⽤原⽂：Women won the right to swim in public pools", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What have researchers and law enforcement agencies tried to do?", "options": ["A) Build a machine that can detect lies.", "B) Develop a magnetic brain scanner.", "C) Test the credibility of court evidence.", "D) Win people's complete trust in them."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Build a machine that can detect lies.\n【定位】For decades, researchers and law enforcement agencies have tried to build a machine that will\ndo the same thing.\n几十年来，研究人员和执法机构一直试图制造一台能做到同样事情的机器。\n【信号】题干主语原样出现。这一句把题干里的主语 researchers and law enforcement agencies\n与动词 have tried to build a machine 原样念出，主语与\"tried to\"同时落地，是答案句最\n硬的定位信号；其中 do the same thing 回指上文\"识破谎言\"，说明这台机器就是用来测\n谎的。\n【替换】build a machine that will do the same thing ↔ Build a machine that can detect lies.\n代词回指还原成本义\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 Develop a magnetic brain scanner. 借用了录音第二段 magnetic brain scans 这个\n原词，听到\"磁性脑扫描\"就想选；但开发脑扫描仪的是马萨诸塞那家\"公司\"，不是题干问\n的\"研究人员和执法机构\"，主体张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：by using magnetic brain scans\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Test the credibility of court evidence. 把录音里 credible proof 的 credible 和后文\nthe courts 这两个原词拼到一起，制造\"听过\"的熟悉感；但录音从未说研究人员去\"检验法\n庭证据的可信度\"，是用原词凑出的无中生有。\n借⽤原⽂：has not provided credible proof\nD)\n选项 Win people's complete trust in them. 蹭了\"trust\"这一全段反复出现的主题词，但研\n究人员和执法机构的尝试是\"造测谎机器\"，不是\"赢得人们的完全信任\"，把动作偷换成了一\n个录音没提的目的。\n借⽤原⽂：machines to spot deception", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "How do many brain scientists respond to the Massachusetts company's so-called technological breakthrough?", "options": ["A) They are optimistic about its potential.", "B) They are sceptical of its reliability.", "C) They think it is but business promotion.", "D) They celebrate it with great enthusiasm."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） They are sceptical of its reliability.\n【定位】You'll not find many brain scientists celebrating this breakthrough. The company might\nbe very optimistic, but the ability of their machine to detect deception has not provided\ncredible proof.\n你不会看到多少脑科学家为这一突破欢呼。那家公司也许非常乐观，但他们的机器识破谎言\n的能力并没有提供可信的证据。\n【信号】否定+转折but点出真态度。前半 not find many brain scientists celebrating 用否定先排\n除\"庆祝\"，紧接 The company might be very optimistic, but… 的转折 but 把乐观归给公\n司、把\"没有可信证据\"留给科学家——转折之后才是科学家的真实态度：怀疑其可靠性。\n【替换】has not provided credible proof ↔ They are sceptical of its reliability.\n否定式陈述=怀疑态度\nnot find many brain scientists celebrating this breakthrough ↔\nThey are sceptical of its reliability.\n不庆祝=不看好\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n选项 They are optimistic about its potential. 直接搬用录音原词 optimistic，听到就想\n选；但原文是 The company might be very optimistic——乐观的是\"公司\"，不是题干问\n的\"脑科学家\"，态度被安错了主体。\n借⽤原⽂：The company might be very optimistic\nC)\n选项 They think it is but business promotion. 把科学家的态度拔高成\"不过是商业炒\n作\"；录音只说\"未提供可信证据、需更多研究\"，是\"存疑\"而非断定其为商业骗局，程度被推\n过头。\n借⽤原⽂：Much more research is badly needed.\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 They celebrate it with great enthusiasm. 蹭了原词 celebrating，但录音是 not\nfind many…celebrating——加了否定，意思正好相反，听到 celebrate 就选会掉进\"漏听\n否定\"的反向陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：You'll not find many brain scientists celebrating this breakthrough.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does the speaker think of using a high-tech test to determine whether a person is telling the truth?", "options": ["A) It is not to be trusted at all.", "B) It does not sound economical.", "C) It may intrude into people's privacy.", "D) It may lead to overuse in court trials."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） It is not to be trusted at all.\n【定位】A high-tech test that can tell when a person is not telling the truth sounds too good to be true.\nAnd when something sounds too good to be true, it usually is.\n一项能识破谎言的高科技测试听上去好得不像真的。而当某样东西听上去好得不像真的时\n候，它通常就真的不是真的。\n【信号】结尾收束的总评句。全文最后一段是说话人的收束总评，习语 too good to be true 加上 it\nusually is（言下之意\"它通常就是假的、不可信\"）直接给出说话人对这项技术的最终判断\n——不可信，是典型\"结尾下结论\"的答案信号。\n【替换】when something sounds too good to be true, it usually is ↔ It is not to be trusted at all.\n习语弦外之意=完全不可信\nProblems surround the use of machines to spot deception ↔ It is not to be trusted at all.\n问题缠身=不可信\n【排除】B) 未提及\n选项 It does not sound economical. 谈\"不划算/不经济\"，录音通篇讨论的是可靠性、隐\n私与司法，从未提及成本或经济性，属无中生有。\nC)\n选项 It may intrude into people's privacy. 听上去很像测谎技术的常见担忧，但录音批的\n是\"可靠性未经检验、问题缠身\"，并未谈\"侵犯隐私\"，是用常识脑补出的岔题。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 It may lead to overuse in court trials. 蹭了录音里 the courts 这一原词，但说话人\n担心的是\"法官别被机器骗\"，并非\"会在法庭被过度使用\"，用原词拼出了录音没下的结论。\n借⽤原⽂：Let's hope they're wise enough not to be fooled by a machine", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the island of Makelua?", "options": ["A) Most of its residents speak several languages.", "B) Some of its indigenous languages are dying out.", "C) Each village there speaks a totally different language.", "D) Its languages have interested researchers the world over."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Each village there speaks a totally different language.\n【定位】Yet the residents of each village speak a completely different language.\n然而，每个村庄的居民说的都是一种完全不同的语言。\n【信号】转折词 Yet 后才是落点。前一句说“站在一个村边就能看到下一个村”，Yet 一转，强调“即便\n挨得这么近，每村语言却完全不同”——转折之后的这句才是说话人真正要讲的事实，正是\n答案句。\n【替换】the residents of each village speak a completely different language ↔\nEach village there speaks a totally different language\ncompletely=totally 的同义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nMost of its residents speak several languages. 借用了原文反复出现的 residents 和\nlanguages，但原文说的是“各村各说一种不同语言”，不是“一个人会说好几种语言”，听到\n原词就选会中招。\n借⽤原⽂：the residents of each village speak a completely different language\nB) 未提及\nSome of its indigenous languages are dying out. 里 indigenous languages 确在原文\n出现（40 different indigenous languages），但全段只说岛上语言“多”，根本没提任何语\n言“正在消亡”，属凭空添加。\n借⽤原⽂：40 different indigenous languages\nD) 过度推断\nIts languages have interested researchers the world over. 用了 researchers（原文确有\nmy colleagues at the Max Planck Institute），但原文只是引用同事的研究数据，并未说\n“全世界研究者都对这岛的语言感兴趣”，是过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：my colleagues at the Max Planck Institute for the Science of Human History", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What do we learn from the talk about languages in the world?", "options": ["A) They are spread randomly across the world.", "B) Some are more difficult to learn than others.", "C) More are found in tropical regions than in the mild zones.", "D) They enrich and impact each other in more ways than one."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） More are found in tropical regions than in the mild zones.\n【定位】And these languages are not spread randomly across the planet. For example, far more\nlanguages are found in tropical regions than in the mild zones.\n而且这些语言并非随机地分布在地球上。例如，热带地区的语言远多于温带地区。\n【信号】否定句后接 For example 的举例。not spread randomly 先给出否定性结论——分布并非\n随机，紧接着 For example 用“热带远多于温带”来佐证；结论句加举例句正是答案落点。\n【替换】far more languages are found in tropical regions than in the mild zones ↔\nMore are found in tropical regions than in the mild zones\n近乎原句保留的比较结构\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey are spread randomly across the world. 几乎照搬原文的 spread randomly，但原\n文是 not spread randomly——带否定。漏听 not 就会选成与原文相反的 A。\n借⽤原⽂：these languages are not spread randomly across the planet\nB) 未提及\nSome are more difficult to learn than others. 讲“有的语言更难学”，原文通篇讲的是数量\n与分布，从未涉及语言的学习难易，属凭空添加。\nD) 答非所问\nThey enrich and impact each other in more ways than one. 借了 languages 这个高频\n主语，但原文谈的是“语言分布不均”，没说语言之间“相互丰富、相互影响”，是另起话题的\n过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：these languages are not spread randomly across the planet", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What has the diverse team of researchers found about the previous studies on language diversity?", "options": ["A) They used different methods to collect and analyze data.", "B) They identified distinct patterns of language distribution.", "C) Their conclusions do not correspond to their original hypotheses.", "D) There is no conclusive account for the cause of language diversity."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） There is no conclusive account for the cause of language diversity.\n【定位】The results varied a lot from one study to another, and no clear patterns emerged.\n各项研究的结果差异很大，没有出现清晰的规律。\n【信号】否定结论句 no clear patterns。no clear patterns emerged 是对以往研究的总结性否定\n——结果各异、没有规律，再加上前文 we have few clear answers，共同点明“成因至今\n没有定论”，是答案落点。\n【替换】no clear patterns emerged ↔ There is no conclusive account for the cause of language diversity\nno clear patterns 概括为 no conclusive account\ncorrelation does not equal causation ↔ the cause of language diversity\n由“相关不等于因果”落到“成因未明”\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey used different methods to collect and analyze data. 借了原文 methodological\n的词根，但原文说的是这些研究遇到“方法学难题”（methodological challenges），不是\n“用了不同方法收集分析数据”，听到 method 类词就选会中招。\n借⽤原⽂：methodological challenges\nB) 与原文相反\nThey identified distinct patterns of language distribution. 说“找到了清晰的分布规律”，\n恰与原文 no clear patterns emerged“没有清晰规律”相反，盗用 patterns 一词却反了结\n论。\n借⽤原⽂：no clear patterns emerged\nC) 过度推断\nTheir conclusions do not correspond to their original hypotheses. 里 hypotheses 确\n在原文出现（They hypothesize），但那是说“普通人会去假设成因”，原文并未说“研究结\n论与原假设不符”，是借原词的过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：They hypothesize that language diversity must be about history", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What do the surveys show about America according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) Its middle-class is disappearing.", "B) Its wealth is rationally distributed.", "C) Its population is rapidly growing.", "D) Its cherished dream is coming true."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Its middle-class is disappearing.\n【定位】Today's demographics show that the middle-class is disappearing and now the richest 1% of\nthe population has amassed more wealth than the bottom 90%.\n当今的人口数据显示，中产阶级正在消失，如今最富有的百分之一人口所积累的财富已超过\n最底层百分之九十的人。\n【信号】show that 引出结论。Today's demographics show that the middle-class is\ndisappearing 中的 demographics show that 与题干 surveys show 对应，是直接亮出调\n查结论的信号句，show that 后面跟的就是答案。\n【替换】the middle-class is disappearing ↔ Its middle-class is disappearing.\n几乎原句对应\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n选项说 Its wealth is rationally distributed.（财富合理分配），但录音说 the richest 1%\nof the population has amassed more wealth than the bottom 90%，是财富高度集\n中、极不合理，方向正相反。\n借⽤原⽂：the richest 1% of the population has amassed more wealth than the bottom 90%\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Its population is rapidly growing. 谈人口快速增长，录音虽出现 population 一词，\n但只说财富在 1% 与底层 90% 间的分布，从未说人口在快速增长，是借 population 制造\n的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：the richest 1% of the population\nD) 与原文相反\n选项说 Its cherished dream is coming true.（美国梦正在实现），但录音开篇就问 is the\ndream as we once knew it dying?（这梦是否正在死去），全篇基调是梦在褪色，与梦想\n成真正相反。\n借⽤原⽂：is the dream as we once knew it dying?", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What did Americans use to believe?", "options": ["A) Success was but a dream without conscientious effort.", "B) They could realize their dreams through hard work.", "C) A few dollars could go a long way.", "D) Wealth was shared by all citizens."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They could realize their dreams through hard work.\n【定位】Once upon a time, Americans thought that if they worked hard enough, even in the face of\nadversity, they would be rewarded with success.\n曾几何时，美国人认为只要足够努力，哪怕身处逆境，也终将被回报以成功。\n【信号】Once upon a time 标过去。Once upon a time, Americans thought that 中 Once\nupon a time + thought 是典型的过去信念信号，与题干 used to believe 对应，其后从句\n即答案。\n【替换】if they worked hard enough ↔ through hard work\n句式换说法\nthey would be rewarded with success ↔ realize their dreams\n成功=实现梦想\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n选项 Success was but a dream without conscientious effort. 用 but a dream（不过是\n空想）、without effort 把句子绕成成功没努力就只是空想，盗用了 success、dream、\neffort 等原词，但录音讲的是努力会得到回报的正面信念，A 的绕法改了原意。\n借⽤原⽂：if they worked hard enough\nC) 张冠李戴\n选项 A few dollars could go a long way.（一点钱也能顶大用）盗用了录音后文 a little\ncompassion goes a long way 的 go a long way 句式，但原文说的是一点同情心顶用、\n且属第二段讲现状，与本题问的过去信念不是一回事。\n借⽤原⽂：a little compassion goes a long way\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Wealth was shared by all citizens. 说财富人人共享，录音 wealth 出现过，但只讲财\n富被极少数人占有，从未说过去人人共享，是 all 绝对化加原词 wealth 拼出的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：the richest 1% of the population has amassed more wealth than the bottom 90%", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What do low-income people aspire to?", "options": ["A) Better working conditions.", "B) Better-paying jobs.", "C) High social status.", "D) Full employment."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Full employment.\n【定位】And if you ask low-income people what's the one thing that will change their life, they'll say a\n\"full-time job.\" That's all they aspire to.\n如果你问低收入者什么东西能改变他们的生活，他们会说是一份全职工作。那就是他们全部\n的渴望。\n【信号】aspire to 原词直答。they'll say a \"full-time job.\" That's all they aspire to 中 aspire to\n与题干完全同词，前面紧跟的 full-time job 就是他们渴望的东西，是直接应答信号。\n【替换】full-time job ↔ Full employment.\n全职=充分就业\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 Better working conditions.（更好的工作条件）听来合理，但录音里低收入者明确只\n说想要 full-time job，从未提到改善工作条件，属凭常识脑补的未提及项。\nB) 张冠李戴\n选项 Better-paying jobs.（高薪工作）盗用了第三段 well-paying blue-collar jobs 的字\n眼，但那是讲技术与全球化减少了高薪蓝领岗，属客观背景，不是低收入者本题所表达的渴\n望对象。\n借⽤原⽂：well-paying blue-collar jobs\nC) 未提及\n选项 High social status.（高社会地位）录音通篇未涉及社会地位这一维度，是话题外的无\n中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What do businesses do to increase their revenues?", "options": ["A) Reduce the administrative costs.", "B) Adopt effective business models.", "C) Hire part-time employees only.", "D) Make use of the latest technology."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Hire part-time employees only.\n【定位】Businesses, in order to boost profits, hire employees as part-time workers only.\n企业为了提升利润，只把员工当作兼职工来雇用。\n【信号】in order to boost profits。in order to boost profits, hire employees as part-time\nworkers only 中 in order to boost profits 是与题干 increase revenues 对应的目的信\n号，其后的动作 hire…part-time workers only 即答案。\n【替换】hire employees as part-time workers only ↔ Hire part-time employees only.\n近乎原句\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 Reduce the administrative costs.（降低管理成本）是企业增利的常见手段，但录音\n从未提到削减管理成本，属常识脑补的未提及项。\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 Adopt effective business models. 用了 business models，呼应录音 profit-driven\nbusiness models，但录音是把这种逐利模式当作问题成因来批评，并非说企业靠采用有效\n模式增利，B 把批评对象偷换成正面手段。\n借⽤原⽂：profit-driven business models\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Make use of the latest technology. 盗用第三段 technological advancement 的\ntechnology，但那句讲的是技术进步减少了高薪蓝领岗这一背景，并非企业为增利主动采\n用技术的做法，与本题问的手段不符。\n借⽤原⽂：technological advancement and a global economy have reduced the demand for well-\npaying blue-collar jobs", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aptitude", "B) eradication", "C) evaluated", "D) evaporated", "E) filtering", "F) grasp", "G) legacy", "H) minimize", "I) obscure", "J) obsess", "K) raising", "L) resist", "M) significant", "N) suffer", "O) typical"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) grasp\n动词\n【词性槽】不定式 to 之后、宾语 the material 之前的动词原形\n【依据】grasp the material = 掌握课程内容，是地道学习搭配\n上文 hurts students who're already struggling to __(26)__ the material. 在讲那些 \"本就\n吃力的学生\"，他们费力做的事正是 \"理解、掌握教学内容\"。grasp 本义 \"抓住\"，引申为\n\"理解\"，与 the material（课程材料）形成英语写作中最常见的搭配之一。\n【竞争词】J) obsess 搭配不成立：obsess 不直接接 the material\nobsess 常用结构是 be obsessed with sth，不会用作 struggle to obsess the material；也不\n符合 \"学生想搞懂内容\" 的语义指向。\nL) resist 义不对路：抵抗教材不通\nresist 指 \"抵抗、抵制\"，struggle to resist the material 字面意为 \"努力抵制教学内容\"，与 \"本\n就吃力的学生需要帮助\" 的语境完全反向。\nN) suffer 搭配/义双不合：suffer 一般不带 the material 这种宾语\nsuffer 作 \"遭受\" 多接苦难/损失，suffer the material 在英语中不成话；且本段聚焦 \"理解吃\n力\"，而非 \"遭受\" 之意。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·不定式 to 之后、宾语 the mat"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aptitude", "B) eradication", "C) evaluated", "D) evaporated", "E) filtering", "F) grasp", "G) legacy", "H) minimize", "I) obscure", "J) obsess", "K) raising", "L) resist", "M) significant", "N) suffer", "O) typical"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) evaluated\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 researchers 后缺谓语，要过去式及物动词\n【依据】evaluate students = 评估/考察学生，是研究行为的标配动词\n句首 To measure the effects 已经把研究意图点出来——要 \"衡量上网走神的影响\"，研究\n者自然 \"对 500 名学生进行评估/考察\"。evaluate 在研究语境中专指 \"评估、考察、做研究\n意义上的评定\"，与 measure / study 同义场。\n【竞争词】D) evaporated 义反人话：让 500 个学生 \"蒸发\" 不成立\nevaporate 本义 \"蒸发\"，引申为 \"（希望/计划）消失\"，作及物用时也只接 liquid 等具体对象，\n不能以 500 students 作宾语。这是典型的 \"形近 + 形态对、但语义不对\" 干扰。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语 researchers 后缺谓语，"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aptitude", "B) eradication", "C) evaluated", "D) evaporated", "E) filtering", "F) grasp", "G) legacy", "H) minimize", "I) obscure", "J) obsess", "K) raising", "L) resist", "M) significant", "N) suffer", "O) typical"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) aptitude\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 intellectual 后的中心词位\n【依据】intellectual aptitude = 智力水平，正是 ACT 衡量的对象\nACT 是美国大学入学考试，被研究者拿来当 \"智力高低\" 的代理指标，intellectual aptitude\n（智力天赋/能力）是英文里描述这一概念的高频搭配。下文 people with high intellectual\nabilities are better at __(29)__ out distractions 紧跟着用 intellectual abilities 复述，几\n乎等同于把答案明牌。\n【竞争词】B) eradication 词义南辕北辙：根除与智力无搭配\neradication 指 \"根除（疾病、贫困等）\"，intellectual eradication 在英语里不存在，硬填会让\n整句失语义。\nG) legacy 词义偏向遗产/遗留，与衡量智力无关\nlegacy 强调 \"前人遗留之物\"，intellectual legacy 偶见，但指 \"思想遗产\"（如某位思想家的影\n响），不是 ACT 这种 \"测智力高低\" 的测量对象。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 intellectual 后的中"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aptitude", "B) eradication", "C) evaluated", "D) evaporated", "E) filtering", "F) grasp", "G) legacy", "H) minimize", "I) obscure", "J) obsess", "K) raising", "L) resist", "M) significant", "N) suffer", "O) typical"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) filtering\n动词\n【词性槽】介词 at 之后的动名词位\n【依据】filter out distractions = 过滤掉干扰，与全篇 \"抗走神\" 主旨严丝合缝\nfilter out 是英语里描述 \"把干扰、噪音筛掉\" 的固定动词短语；本句要解释 \"高智力者更擅\n长什么\"，对应正是 \"过滤掉外界干扰\"，与全文反复出现的 distractions 主题词形成同框。\n【竞争词】K) raising raise out 不存在，且义反向\nraise 不与 out 构成固定短语；语义上 \"提起干扰\" 与 \"高智力者擅长应对干扰\" 相反——本句要\n的是 \"消除/屏蔽\"，不是 \"提起\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 at 之后的动名词位"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aptitude", "B) eradication", "C) evaluated", "D) evaporated", "E) filtering", "F) grasp", "G) legacy", "H) minimize", "I) obscure", "J) obsess", "K) raising", "L) resist", "M) significant", "N) suffer", "O) typical"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) significant\n形容词\n【词性槽】冠词 a 与名词 decrease 之间，必填形容词\n【依据】与 \"不显示出明显的下滑\" 的研究预期完全合拍\n上文 people with high intellectual abilities are better at __(29)__ out distractions 是研\n究人员的前提假设，他们由此推断高 ACT 分学生 \"不会出现明显下滑\"。significant\ndecrease（显著下滑）正是学术研究里描述效应是否值得在意的标准搭配，扣住 \"would\nnot show a … decrease\" 这条逻辑。\n【竞争词】I) obscure 义不对路：晦涩的下滑不成立\nobscure 多指 \"晦涩难懂的\"，用来形容数据/趋势下滑在搭配上和语义上都不通，研究者关心的\n是 \"幅度大不大\"，不是 \"看不看得懂\"。\nO) typical 逻辑反向：典型的下滑等于承认会下滑\n若填 typical decrease，意思变成 \"会出现一次典型的下滑\"，恰好与研究者 \"高分学生不会下滑\"\n的假设相反；而原句用了 would not show，需要的是一个 \"程度\" 形容词来削弱、不是 \"种类\"\n形容词来确认。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 O) —— typical decrease 看似自然，实则把研究假设说反。考生很\n容易因为 typical 读起来 \"顺口\" 就填进 would not show a __(30)__ decrease in\nperformance；但研究者的假设是 \"高智力者不会出现明显下滑\"，需要的是程度副形\nsignificant，而非种类形 typical。防坑口诀：would not show a ___ decrease，要的是\n\"明不明显\"，不是 \"是哪种\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·冠词 a 与名词 decrease 之间"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aptitude", "B) eradication", "C) evaluated", "D) evaporated", "E) filtering", "F) grasp", "G) legacy", "H) minimize", "I) obscure", "J) obsess", "K) raising", "L) resist", "M) significant", "N) suffer", "O) typical"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) raising\n动词\n【词性槽】are 后缺谓语补足，要现在分词构成进行时\n【依据】raise alarm bells = 敲响警钟，是英语高频习语\nraise the alarm / raise alarm bells 表示 \"对某种危险/问题发出警告\"，正合本段 \"教授们\n越来越多地对手机/笔电/平板的影响敲响警钟\" 之意，与后文 80% 学生上课用手机、25%\n承认成绩受损的数据形成 \"警告 → 实证\" 的内部呼应。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·are 后缺谓语补足，要现在分词构成进行"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aptitude", "B) eradication", "C) evaluated", "D) evaporated", "E) filtering", "F) grasp", "G) legacy", "H) minimize", "I) obscure", "J) obsess", "K) raising", "L) resist", "M) significant", "N) suffer", "O) typical"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) typical\n形容词\n【词性槽】冠词 a 与名词 class 之间的形容词位\n【依据】average student 与 typical class 互为呼应\n上文用 the average student（一般的学生）做主语，要的是与之相呼应的 \"一节常规\n课\"——typical class 是地道搭配，意为 \"平均每节课一般会查 11 次手机\"，把 \"普通学生\"\n与 \"普通课堂\" 的统计抽象配齐。\n【竞争词】I) obscure 晦涩的课与统计语境不搭\nobscure class 字面是 \"晦涩难懂的课\"，与 \"average student … 11 times\" 这种统计陈述完全\n脱节；本句要描述 \"普遍/典型现象\"，不是给某节难懂的课贴标签。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·冠词 a 与名词 class 之间的形容"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aptitude", "B) eradication", "C) evaluated", "D) evaporated", "E) filtering", "F) grasp", "G) legacy", "H) minimize", "I) obscure", "J) obsess", "K) raising", "L) resist", "M) significant", "N) suffer", "O) typical"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) suffer\n动词\n【词性槽】cause sb/sth to do 结构里的不定式动词原形\n【依据】grades suffer = 成绩下滑，是英语里描述 \"成绩受损\" 的地道说法\n本段 A quarter of students report that their use of digital devices during class causes\ntheir grades to __(33)__. 在讲 \"四分之一学生承认数字设备拖累了成绩\"，suffer 的不及物\n用法可以直接以 grades/health/business 为主语，意为 \"受损/变差\"，正中靶心。\n【竞争词】H) minimize 及物动词且语义反向\nminimize 是及物动词（minimize sth），不能以 grades 作主语自动 \"被最小化\"；即使硬解\n读，语义也是 \"使最小化\"，与 \"被拖累而下滑\" 不对。\nJ) obsess 义不对：成绩不会 \"着迷\"\nobsess 指 \"使着迷\"，与 grades 搭配在语义上不成立——成绩不是有意识的主体，不\n能 obsess。\nL) resist 义反向：成绩 \"抵抗\" 没在说人话\nresist 要的是有意志的主体；让 grades 去 \"抵抗\" 既不通搭配又与 \"成绩因走神而下滑\" 的因果\n反着来。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·cause sb/sth to do 结"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aptitude", "B) eradication", "C) evaluated", "D) evaporated", "E) filtering", "F) grasp", "G) legacy", "H) minimize", "I) obscure", "J) obsess", "K) raising", "L) resist", "M) significant", "N) suffer", "O) typical"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) minimize\n动词\n【词性槽】不定式 to designed to do 中的动词原形\n【依据】minimize students' use = 把学生的使用降到最低，正是政策意图\n后文 some instructors even confiscate(没收) tablets and phones. 把 \"老师没收设备\" 当\n成例子，说明前面那个动词应当表达 \"减少、抑制\" 学生用设备——minimize 完全对得上\n\"政策旨在最小化设备使用\" 的句意。\n【竞争词】J) obsess 义反人话：让政策 \"使学生着迷于用设备\"\nobsess 表 \"使着迷\"，policies designed to obsess students' use 在逻辑上让 \"政策反过来鼓\n励沉迷\"，与原意彻底相反。\nL) resist 搭配错位：抵制 \"学生的使用\" 不是政策本身的动作\nresist 指 \"抵制/抵抗\"，但政策本身并不会去 \"抵抗\" 某种行为——它更可能 \"减少/限制/规范\" 该\n行为。此外，下文 Until students are able to __(35)__ the pull of social networking… 才是真\n正的 resist 出场位置，留给它更合适。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 L) —— designed to resist students' use 看着也通其实搭配错\n位。考生看到 \"对抗手机依赖\" 的全段主旨，容易把 resist 提前用到 34 题；但政策本\n身去 \"抵制\" 学生的使用搭配不自然，且会挤掉 35 题最契合的 resist the pull。防坑口\n诀：resist 留给 the pull / temptation 这种 \"诱惑\" 类宾语，policies 的目的动词更适\n合 minimize/reduce/limit。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·不定式 to designed to d"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aptitude", "B) eradication", "C) evaluated", "D) evaporated", "E) filtering", "F) grasp", "G) legacy", "H) minimize", "I) obscure", "J) obsess", "K) raising", "L) resist", "M) significant", "N) suffer", "O) typical"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) resist\n动词\n【词性槽】be able to + 动词原形\n【依据】resist the pull of … = 抵御……的诱惑/拉力，是地道固定搭配\nthe pull of social networking, texting, and endlessly surfing the web 把 \"社交、短信、\n无尽刷网\" 比作一股 \"拉力/诱惑\"，与 resist 形成 \"抵制诱惑\" 的经典搭配。全段在讲 \"人们\n对手机越来越依赖，要靠学生自己顶住\"，resist 恰好回应这条主线。\n【竞争词】J) obsess 义反向：让学生 \"沉迷于这股拉力\" 与全段相反\nobsess 强调 \"沉迷\"，若填入则成 \"直到学生能沉迷于社交/短信的拉力\"，与紧接着的 \"否则他们\n将继续在课业中挣扎\" 因果倒置，整段逻辑崩塌。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be able to + 动词原形"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Merian was the first scientist to study a type of American ant.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】首次研究某种美洲蚂蚁\n【定位】In the same plate, Merian depicted and described leaf-cutter ants for the first time. \"In\nAmerica there are large ants which can eat whole trees bare as a broom handle in a single\nnight,\" she wrote in the description.\n在同一张插图里，Merian 首次描绘并描述了切叶蚁。她在描述中写道：“在美洲，有一种大\n蚂蚁能在一夜之间把整棵树啃得像扫帚柄一样光秃。”\n【改写】the first scientist to study ↔ depicted and described leaf-cutter ants for the first time\nfirst 同义还原\na type of American ant ↔ In America there are large ants 与 leaf-cutter ants\n上位 → 下位 + 地点同义\n【辨邻】 L L 段确实出现 army and leaf-cutter ants，但说的是“她错把行军蚁和切叶蚁混在一\n起”，对应的是题干 Q43“偶有错误”，不是 Q36“首次研究”。\nincorrectly lumped together army and leaf-cutter ants", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "The European audience was more interested in Merian's drawings than her gender.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】更关心画作而非性别\n【定位】The image, which also contained novel descriptions of ants, fascinated a European audience\nthat was more concerned with the exotic story unfolding before them than the gender of the\nperson who painted it.\n这幅画还附有对蚂蚁的新颖描述，让欧洲读者深为着迷——他们更关心眼前展开的异域故\n事，而不是画家本人的性别。\n【改写】more interested in ↔ more concerned with\n情感动词同义替换\nMerian's drawings ↔ the exotic story unfolding before them\n指代同一对象\nher gender ↔ the gender of the person who painted it\n代词 her 还原成定语从句", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Merian's masterpiece came under attack a century after its publication.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】出版一个世纪后遭攻击\n【定位】In her work, she revealed a side of nature so exotic, dramatic and valuable to Europeans of\nthe time that she received much acclaim. But a century later, her findings came under\nscientific criticism.\n在她的作品中，她揭示了大自然如此奇异、戏剧化且对当时欧洲人极有价值的一面，使她广\n受赞誉。但一个世纪后，她的研究发现遭到了科学界的批评。\n【改写】came under attack ↔ came under scientific criticism\nattack ↔ criticism 同义替换\na century after its publication ↔ a century later\n时间状语同义替换\nMerian's masterpiece ↔ her findings\n代表作 ↔ 研究发现\n【辨邻】 J J 段确实写到她被骂 silly woman 以及 Henry Walter Bates...in a book in 1863 翻\n案，但它是 E 段“百年后受攻击”这件事的延伸细节，不是“作品受攻击”这一事件的首次\n陈述句；题干问的是“何时遭攻击”的首次表述，所以源头段在 E。\nShe'd been called a silly woman for saying that a spider could eat a bird", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Merian's mistakes in her drawings may be attributed to her shortened stay in South America.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】缩短南美停留导致出错\n【定位】Perhaps one explanation for her mistakes is that she cut short her Suriname trip after getting\nsick, and completed the book at home in Amsterdam.\n也许她出错的一个解释是：她因病提前结束了苏里南之行，并回到阿姆斯特丹家中完成了这\n本书。\n【改写】may be attributed to ↔ one explanation for her mistakes is that\n归因表达同义\nher shortened stay in South America ↔ she cut short her Suriname trip\n动 ↔ 名 + 地名上下位\nMerian's mistakes in her drawings ↔ her mistakes\n范围一致\n【辨邻】 L L 段把 incorrectly lumped together army and leaf-cutter ants、she painted\nfour、not every caterpillar and butterfly matched 一一列出，但完全没解释“为什\n么”，归因解释在下一段 M 才出现。\nMerian was correct about the giant bird-eating spiders, ants building bridges with their bodies and\nother details. But in the same drawing, she incorrectly lumped together army and leaf-cutter ants.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Merian often sat up the whole night through to observe and draw insects.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】通宵观察绘画\n【定位】She would sit up all night until they came out of the pupa so she could draw them.\n她常常彻夜守候，直到蝴蝶、毛毛虫破蛹而出，好趁机把它们画下来。\n【改写】sat up the whole night through ↔ sit up all night\n短语同义\nto observe and draw insects ↔ until they came out of the pupa so she could draw them\n动作流程映射\n【辨邻】 H H 段写 The results of her decades' worth of careful observations were detailed\npaintings and descriptions，强调成果与可能借助 a magnifying glass 的细节，未\n提“通宵守候”这一工作方式，不承担 Q40 的“通宵 + 画”。\nThe results of her decades' worth of careful observations were detailed paintings and descriptions of\nEuropean insects", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Merian acknowledged the help she got from natives of South America.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】致谢南美原住民\n【定位】Merian also made note of the help she received from the natives of Suriname, as well as\nslaves or servants that assisted her.\nMerian 还专门记下了她从苏里南原住民那里得到的帮助，以及奴隶或仆人对她的协助。\n【改写】acknowledged the help ↔ made note of the help\n短语同义\nnatives of South America ↔ the natives of Suriname\n地名上下位\n【辨邻】 N N 段写 She received assistance naming plants, making sketches and referencing\nthe work of others. Her daughters helped her color her drawings，致谢对象是合\n作者与女儿，不是 natives；题干指明“原住民”，源头段必须在 O。\nShe received assistance naming plants, making sketches and referencing the work of others. Her\ndaughters helped her color her drawings.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Merian contributed greatly to people's better understanding of medicine and science.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】促进对医学与科学的理解\n【定位】The knowledge she collected over decades didn't just satisfy those curious about nature, but\nalso provided valuable insights into medicine and science.\n她数十年间收集的知识不仅满足了那些对自然好奇的人，还为医学和科学提供了有价值的\n洞见。\n【改写】contributed greatly to ↔ provided valuable insights into\n贡献动词同义\npeople's better understanding of medicine and science ↔\nvaluable insights into medicine and science\n名词化等值\n【辨邻】 B B 段 She didn't do as much to change biology as Charles Darwin, but she was\nsignificant 是把她放进科学家序列做对标评价，未提“医学与科学”的具体贡献，无法承\n担 Q42 的“促进医学与科学理解”。\nShe didn't do as much to change biology as Charles Darwin, but she was significant", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Merian occasionally made mistakes in her drawings of insects and birds.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】昆虫与鸟的绘画偶有错\n【定位】But in the same drawing, she incorrectly lumped together army and leaf-cutter ants. And\ninstead of showing just the typical pair of eggs in a hummingbird nest, she painted four. She\nmade other mistakes in Metamorphosis Insectorum Surinamensium as well: not every\ncaterpillar and butterfly matched.\n但是在同一张画里，她错误地把行军蚁和切叶蚁混在一起。她没有画一窝两枚的典型蜂鸟蛋\n数量，而是画了四枚。她在《苏里南昆虫变态》里还犯过其他错误：并不是每条毛毛虫都和\n对应的蝴蝶相配。\n【改写】occasionally made mistakes in her drawings ↔\nshe incorrectly lumped together army and leaf-cutter ants 与 She made other mistakes in\nMetamorphosis Insectorum Surinamensium as well\n副词 ↔ 错误清单\ninsects and birds ↔ ants 与 in a hummingbird nest\n上位 ↔ 下位双指代\n【辨邻】 M M 段 she cut short her Suriname trip after getting sick, and completed the\nbook at home in Amsterdam 解释错的成因，并把她错误与达尔文、牛顿做比较，但\n具体错处罗列在 L 段；问“哪儿画错”必须落在 L。\nThese errors no more invalidate Ms. Merian's work than do well-known misconceptions published\nby Charles Darwin or Isaac Newton\nH H 段 Some criticized this detail later, saying there was just one tongue, but\nMerian wasn't wrong 是她被误批但其实正确的故事，与“偶有犯错”方向相反。\nSome criticized this detail later, saying there was just one tongue, but Merian wasn't wrong.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Now, Merian's role as a female forerunner in sciences has been re-established.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】女性先驱地位被重新确立\n【定位】Today, the pioneering woman of the sciences has re-emerged.\n今天，这位科学领域的女性先驱已经重新浮现。\n【改写】Now ↔ Today\n时间副词同义\na female forerunner in sciences ↔ the pioneering woman of the sciences\n同义短语\nhas been re-established ↔ has re-emerged\n前缀 re- 共享\n【辨邻】 B B 段 She was a scientist on the level with a lot of people we spend a lot of time\ntalking about 是肯定她的地位评价，缺少“先被遗忘 → 现在被重新确立”的时间转折。\nShe was a scientist on the level with a lot of people we spend a lot of time talking about", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Merian made a long voyage to South America to study jungle insects over three centuries ago.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】三百多年前远航南美研究昆虫\n【定位】After years of pleasing a fascinated audience across Europe with books of detailed\ndescriptions and life-size paintings of familiar insects, in 1699 she sailed with her daughter\nnearly 5,000 miles from the Netherlands to South America to study insects in the jungles of\nwhat is now known as Suriname.\n在多年以详尽描述与等大昆虫画作取悦痴迷的欧洲读者之后，1699 年她带着女儿从荷兰航\n行近 5000 英里来到南美，在今天被称为苏里南的丛林里研究昆虫。\n【改写】made a long voyage to South America ↔\nshe sailed with her daughter nearly 5,000 miles from the Netherlands to South America\n名 ↔ 动 + 量级\nto study jungle insects ↔ to study insects in the jungles\n定语前后置等值\nover three centuries ago ↔ in 1699\n年份反算成相对时间\n【辨邻】 M M 段 she cut short her Suriname trip after getting sick 讲的是这次远航被缩短，\n不是“启程远航”的事件本体；远航这一动作本身的源头在 D。\nshe cut short her Suriname trip after getting sick, and completed the book at home in Amsterdam\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA 介绍 Merian 的身份与多重角色：17 世纪德国出生、生活在荷兰的女性，既要操持家\n务育儿，又以艺术家、植物学家、博物学家、昆虫学家身份建立成功事业。\n信息指纹 身份多重\nB 借 Gettysburg College 生物学家 Etheridge 之口给 Merian 定位科学史地位：与\nDarwin 相比她改写生物学的力度小一些，但同属值得被反复谈论的科学家。\n信息指纹 对标达尔文\nC 总述 Merian 的科学贡献：她发现了此前未知的植物与昆虫事实，帮助破除昆虫自泥\n土自发产生的旧说，知识还为医学和科学提供了有价值的洞见，并首次把昆虫与其栖\n息地（含食物）合到一张生态图里。\n信息指纹 医学科学贡献\nD\n讲她 1699 年携女儿从荷兰航行近 5000 英里到南美（今苏里南）丛林研究昆虫，时年\n52 岁；这次远航成就了她的代表作 Metamorphosis Insectorum Surinamensium。\n信息指纹 远航南美\nE 她的作品起初在欧洲被高度赞誉，但一个世纪后遭到科学界批评；粗糙翻印加上 18-\n19 世纪欧洲女性地位倒退，使她长期被遗忘。\n信息指纹 百年后遭批评\nF 讲今天 Merian 作为科学先驱被重新发现：女性主义者、历史学家、艺术家都称赞\n她，生物学家在重新挖掘她随作品配套的科学文本，逝世三百年之际将于阿姆斯特丹\n召开国际研讨会。\n信息指纹 当下被重新承认\nG\n讲她代表作上月被再版，并回顾她从 20 多岁起的工作：先画无文字的花卉昆虫装饰\n画，后来在家饲养蝴蝶、毛毛虫，常常通宵守着等它们破蛹以便绘制。\n信息指纹 通宵守蛹绘图\nH\n讲她数十年观察的成果——欧洲昆虫的详细绘画与描述，以及非常规的远方昆虫与动\n物图景；以斯芬克斯飞蛾“两根舌头”为例，说明她可能用放大镜捕捉到刚破蛹瞬间的\n半管舌结构，后被误批实则无误。\n信息指纹 飞蛾双舌\nI 讲她在 18 世纪初描绘巨型蜘蛛吞食蜂鸟却未遭非议；那幅画还附有对蚂蚁的新颖描\n述，让欧洲读者更关心异域故事本身而非画家性别。\n信息指纹 蜘蛛吞蜂鸟\nJ 维多利亚时代对她的态度反转：作品被错误翻印，部分观察被斥不可能，她甚至被骂\n“傻女人”；后来达尔文之友 Henry Walter Bates 于 1863 年实地观察并著书，证明她对\n蜘蛛能吃鸟一事是对的。\n信息指纹 Bates 平反\nK\n在同一张图里她首次描绘并描述切叶蚁，称美洲存在能一夜吃光整棵树叶的大蚂蚁；\n她还注意到这些蚂蚁把叶子带到地下喂幼蚁，虽未知它们其实是用叶子培养真菌\n喂幼。\n信息指纹 首记切叶蚁\nL\n讲她虽在多项观察上正确，但在同一张图里把行军蚁与切叶蚁错混；蜂鸟巢里画了四\n枚蛋而非两枚；代表作里也有毛毛虫与蝴蝶不匹配等其他错误。\n信息指纹 图中具体错误\nM\n为这些错误提供一种解释：她在苏里南因病提前结束行程、回阿姆斯特丹补完书；并\n以达尔文、牛顿亦有名错为参照，认为不应放大她的错误。\n信息指纹 因病提前回国\nN\n说明她的绘画启发了后世艺术家与生态学家：1801 年英国博物学家 George Shaw 在\n其著作里把一种树蛙以她命名；并提醒不应把功劳全归她，她也得到过命名植物、绘\n草图、参考他人著作以及女儿上色等帮助。\n信息指纹 树蛙以她命名\nO 讲她在书里记下苏里南原住民、奴隶或仆人对她的帮助，并写下感人段落引述他们关\n于孔雀花种子用于堕胎、不愿生孩为奴、宁可自杀以求来世自由等控诉。\n信息指纹 致谢原住民与奴隶\nP 斯坦福科学史教授 Londa Schiebinger 评价 O 段那段话相当令人惊讶；Etheridge 称她\n“超越她那个时代”。\n信息指纹 超前于时代", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the author say about ordinary Americans?", "options": ["A) They struggle to solve math problems.", "B) They think math is a complex subject.", "C) They find high-level math of little use.", "D) They work hard to learn high-level math."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say about ordinary Americans? 是典型的\"作者就 X 说了什\n么\"问法，答案必须由原文一句具体表述支撑。\n【拆句】Q46 主干 What does the author say，介词短语 about ordinary Americans 限定 say 的\n话题范围，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】While human achievements in mathematics continue to reach new levels of complexity, many\nof us who aren't mathematicians at heart (or engineers by trade) may struggle to remember\nthe last time we used calculus(微积分).\n尽管人类在数学上的成就不断攀至新的复杂高度，我们中间那些天生不是数学家（也不是工\n程师出身）的大多数人，可能很难想起来上一次用微积分是什么时候了。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThey struggle to solve math problems. 把原文 struggle to remember 中的\nstruggle 直接挪用过来，但宾语换成 solve math problems，原文从未说普通人\"做题\n困难\"。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 new levels of complexity 指\"数学这门学科本身\"在攀向新的复杂高度，They\nthink math is a complex subject. 偷把这个客观属性偷换成\"普通美国人主观觉得数学\n复杂\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nmany of us who aren't mathematicians at heart (or engineers by trade) may struggle to remember\nthe last time we used calculus\n↔ They find high-level math of little use.\n预判\"普通人很少用到高等数学\"与 They find high-level math of little use. 在语义上完\n全等值。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey work hard to learn high-level math. 说普通人努力学高等数学，但原文\nstruggle to remember the last time we used calculus 表达的是\"几乎用不上\"，逻辑\n方向相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What is the general complaint about America's math education according to Hacker?", "options": ["A) America is not doing as well as China.", "B) Math professors are not doing a good job.", "C) It doesn't help students develop their literacy.", "D) There has hardly been any innovation for years."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 according to Hacker 限定信息来源为 Hacker，要求回到 Hacker 的具体话语中找\n答案。\n【拆句】Q47 主干 What is the general complaint，介词短语 about America's math education\n限定话题，状语 according to Hacker 限定信息来源，三层修饰但语序自然。\n【定位】We hear on all sides that we're not teaching enough mathematics, and the Chinese are running\nrings around us.\n我们四面八方都听到这样的声音：我们教的数学不够多，中国人正把我们远远甩在后面。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe Chinese are running rings around us ↔ America is not doing as well as China.\n预判\"中国正在领先\"的核心命题，与 America is not doing as well as China. 完全\n等值。\nB) 无中生有\nMath professors are not doing a good job. 把抱怨对象偷换成 Math professors，但\n原文众人抱怨的是\"数学教得不够多\"，没有指责具体教师。\nC) 张冠李戴\nliteracy（识字/素养）与本题无关，把 Hacker 在 numerical illiteracy 的 Q48 区域的\n论点搬来当 Q47 抱怨的内容；Q47 问的是众人抱怨的现状，不是 Hacker 自己的论点。\nD) 无中生有\nThere has hardly been any innovation for years. 编造了一个\"多年没创新\"的抱怨，\n全文 P3–P11 都未提及\"创新/innovation\"这一话题。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does Andrew Hacker's Numeracy 101 aim to do?", "options": ["A) Allow students to learn high-level math step by step.", "B) Enable students to make practical use of basic math.", "C) Lay a solid foundation for advanced math studies.", "D) Help students to develop their analytical abilities."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does Andrew Hacker's Numeracy 101 aim to do? 指向一处具体客观信息，\n由原文一句直接支撑。\n【拆句】Q48 主干 What does Andrew Hacker's Numeracy 101 aim to do，所有格 Andrew\nHacker's 修饰 Numeracy 101，结构无陷阱。\n【定位】Such courses, Hacker argues, are a remedy for the numerical illiteracy of adults who have\ncompleted high-level math like algebra but are unable to calculate the price of, say, a carpet\nby area.\nHacker 认为，这类课程是给数字盲成年人的一剂良方——这些人学过代数等高等数学，却\n连按面积算地毯价格这种事都不会。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nAllow students to learn high-level math step by step. 让学生循序渐进学高等数学；\n但 Hacker 在 advanced algebra and other higher-level math should be cut 主张删\n高等数学，Numeracy 101 是高等数学之外的 alternative，不可能 step by step 学高\n等数学。\nB) ✓ 正确\nare a remedy for the numerical illiteracy of adults who have completed high-level math like\nalgebra but are unable to calculate the price of, say, a carpet by area\n↔ Enable students to make practical use of basic math.\n预判\"让人会用基本数学做日常实事\"与 Enable students to make practical use of\nbasic math. 一致。\nC) 与原文相反\nLay a solid foundation for advanced math studies. 说为\"高等数学打基础\"；但\nNumeracy 101 教的是分析联邦预算、公司财报这类\"公民统计\"，目的就是替代高等数\n学之路，而不是为之打基础。\nD) 偷换概念\nHelp students to develop their analytical abilities. 把 learn to analyze public\ninformation like the federal budget 里的 analyze 直接抓出做成\"培养分析能力\"，但\n课程的真正目的是 a remedy for the numerical illiteracy——治数字盲、让人会用基本\n数学，分析公共信息只是手段而非目的本身。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does Maria Droujkova suggest math teachers do in class?", "options": ["A) Make complex concepts easy to understand.", "B) Start teaching children math at an early age.", "C) Help children work wonders with calculus.", "D) Try to arouse students' curiosity in math."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does Maria Droujkova suggest math teachers do 指向具体人物的具体建\n议，由原文一句直接承载。\n【拆句】Q49 主干 What does Maria Droujkova suggest，宾语从句 math teachers do in class\n省略了从属连词 that，math teachers 是宾从主语、do 是谓语；考生若没看出这是宾语从\n句，可能把 in class 错挂到 suggest 上。\n【定位】Make mathematics more available. Redesign it so it's more accessible to more kinds of\npeople: young children, adults who worry about it, adults who may have had bad experiences.\n\"让数学更易接近，\"Droujkova 说，\"重新设计它，让它对更多种人都更易于亲近：小孩、\n对数学有畏惧的成人、有过糟糕经历的成人。\"\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nRedesign it so it's more accessible to more kinds of people: young children, adults who worry\nabout it, adults who may have had bad experiences\n↔ Make complex concepts easy to understand.\n预判\"让数学（包括复杂概念）变得易被各类人理解\"，与 Make complex concepts\neasy to understand. 一致；Droujkova 立场是\"高等数学要保留 + 重设计得让人易亲\n近\"，正对应\"把复杂概念变易懂\"。\nB) 以偏概全\nStart teaching children math at an early age. 只抓住 taught basic calculus\nconcepts to 5-year-olds 这一例证细节，但那只是介绍 Droujkova 个人经历，不是她\n给教师的建议；她的建议是 Redesign it ... more accessible to more kinds of\npeople，对象包括儿童和各种成人，不限于\"早教孩子\"。\nC) 偷换概念\nHelp children work wonders with calculus. 中的 work wonders（创造奇迹）原文无\n对应；它把 P7 介绍 Droujkova 教 5 岁孩子微积分这一事实，偷换成\"让孩子用微积分\n创造奇迹\"，含义被夸张了。\nD) 张冠李戴\nTry to arouse students' curiosity in math. 用的是 an injection of childlike wonder\n中\"孩童般的好奇\"这层意思——但那是 Droujkova 对\"美国课堂\"的总体期望（行文走\n向），不是她给教师的 in class 具体可执行建议；P8 才是\"在课上该做什么\"的祈使句\n段，问题里 in class 把答案锚定到 P8。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does Pamela Harris think should be the goal of math education?", "options": ["A) To enable learners to understand the world better.", "B) To help learners to tell fake math from real math.", "C) To broaden Americans' perspectives on math.", "D) To exert influence on world development."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does Pamela Harris think should be the goal of math education? 指向\nHarris 的具体看法，由原文一句直接支撑。\n【拆句】Q50 主干 What does Pamela Harris think，think 后嵌套主语从句 should be the goal\nof math education，what 同时充当从句的表语；句式有点绕，但语义指向明确——找\nHarris 认为数学教育的目标。\n【定位】Harris says that American education is suffering from an epidemic of \"fake math\"—an\nemphasis on rote memorization(死记硬背) of formulas and steps, rather than an\nunderstanding of how math can influence the ways we see the world.\nHarris 说，美国教育正在遭受一种\"假数学\"的流行病——强调对公式和步骤的死记硬背，而\n不是去理解数学如何影响我们看世界的方式。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nTo enable learners to understand the world better. 把宾语从 perspectives on\nmath 偷换成 the world——Harris 强调的是数学对\"看世界方式\"的影响（让人对\"数学\n如何影响视角\"有理解），不是让人去\"更好地理解世界\"本身；目标的关注点是\nmath/perspectives on math，不是 the world。\nB) 无中生有\nTo help learners to tell fake math from real math. 把 fake math 这个 Harris 用来批\n评现状的词，偷做成\"教育目标=让人区分假数学与真数学\"；但 Harris 从未把\"区分真假\n数学\"当成教育目标，fake math 只是她给现状贴的诊断标签。\nC) ✓ 正确\nan understanding of how math can influence the ways we see the world ↔\nTo broaden Americans' perspectives on math.\n预判\"让人懂数学如何影响我们看世界\"与 To broaden Americans' perspectives on\nmath. 在语义上一致：对数学的视角被拓宽=理解数学如何影响我们看世界。注：标准答\n案为 C。\nD) 过度推断\nTo exert influence on world development. 把 influence the ways we see the world\n（影响\"看世界的方式\"）夸张成\"影响世界发展\"——influence 的宾语由 ways we see\nthe world（认知层面）被偷换成 world development（现实层面），推过了头。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author say about Japan?", "options": ["A) It delivers the best medications for the elderly.", "B) It takes the lead in providing robotic care.", "C) It provides retraining for registered nurses.", "D) It sets the trend in future robotics technology."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say about Japan? 是 \"What does the author say about +\n具体对象\"，问的是文中对某个具体对象的客观陈述，不带 infer/suggest/imply。\n【拆句】What does the author say about Japan? 是标准 wh- 疑问句：主语 the author，谓语\nsay，about Japan 是介词短语状语指明话题。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Japan is ahead of the curve when it comes to this trend.\n在这一趋势上，日本走在了前列。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nIt delivers the best medications for the elderly. 把 deliver medications 偷换成\"最好\n的药\"，并增加\"for the elderly\"，原文没有任何\"最好\"或\"老人\"对应表达。\nB) ✓ 正确\nJapan is ahead of the curve when it comes to this trend. ↔\nIt takes the lead in providing robotic care.\nB 项把\"领先\"用 take the lead 表达，把\"机器人护理\"用 robotic care 表达，与预判完全\n契合，故选 B。\nC) 无中生有\nIt provides retraining for registered nurses. 中的 retraining 在全文找不到对应；原\n文仅提护士短缺，未提日本对护士做再培训。\nD) 以偏概全\nIt sets the trend in future robotics technology. 把原文限定的\"机器人护理 (this\ntrend = 用机器人解决护士短缺)\"扩大为\"整个未来机器人技术\"，范围过大。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What do we learn about the robot Terapio?", "options": ["A) It has been put to use in many Japanese hospitals.", "B) It provides specific individualized care to patients.", "C) It does not have much direct contact with patients.", "D) It has not revolutionized medical service in Japan."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn about the robot Terapio? 问 \"我们从文中了解到 Terapio 什\n么\"，是 What do we learn about 句型，等同于细节询问。\n【拆句】What do we learn about the robot Terapio? 主语 we，谓语 learn，about the robot\nTerapio 是话题状语，the robot 是 Terapio 的同位说明。无难点。\n【定位】This type of robot will likely be one of the first to be implemented in hospitals because it has\nfairly minimal patient contact.\n这类机器人很可能会成为最早被引入医院的机器人之一，因为它与病人的直接接触相当少。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIt has been put to use in many Japanese hospitals. 用现在完成时 has been put to\nuse 表示\"已被广泛使用\"，原文却说 will likely be one of the first to be implemented\n——是将来时的\"很可能将率先被引入\"，并未发生。\nB) 张冠李戴\nIt provides specific individualized care to patients. 中 individualized care to\npatients=\"为病人提供个性化照护\"；原文 It follows a specific individual, such as\na doctor or nurse 是\"跟随某位医护人员\"——主语虽是 Terapio，宾语却是医护而非\n病人。\nC) ✓ 正确\nit has fairly minimal patient contact ↔ It does not have much direct contact with patients.\nC 把 minimal 用 not much 这一更口语化的否定结构表达，把名词短语 patient\ncontact 改成介词结构 contact with patients，与预判逐字对应。\nD) 无中生有\nIt has not revolutionized medical service in Japan. 给 Terapio 下\"尚未革新医疗\"的\n判断，原文只描述其功能，未做任何\"革命/未革命\"的评价。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What are telepresence robots designed to do?", "options": ["A) Directly interact with patients to prevent them from feeling lonely.", "B) Cater to the needs of patients for recovering their cognitive capacity.", "C) Closely monitor the patients' movements and conditions around the clock.", "D) Facilitate communication between patients and doctors or family members."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What are telepresence robots designed to do? 询问该机器人被设计的具体功能，\n是对具体功能的客观提问。\n【拆句】What are telepresence robots designed to do? 主语 telepresence robots，be\ndesigned to do 是被动+不定式，表\"被设计来做……\"。结构清晰。\n【定位】Telepresence robots such as MantaroBot, Vgo, and Giraff can be controlled through a\ncomputer, smartphone, or tablet, allowing family members or doctors to remotely monitor\npatients or Skype them, often via a screen where the robot's 'face' would be.\n诸如 MantaroBot、Vgo、Giraff 之类的远程呈现机器人，可以通过电脑、手机或平板控\n制，让家人或医生远程监护病人，或借助机器人\"脸部\"位置的屏幕与病人 Skype 视频。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nDirectly interact with patients to prevent them from feeling lonely. 中 directly\ninteract 与原文 the robot itself doesn't have to engage directly—it can serve as\nan intermediary 正相反——原文强调机器人不直接参与，只做中介。\nB) 过度推断\nCater to the needs of patients for recovering their cognitive capacity. 中\nrecovering cognitive capacity 暗示\"治疗/恢复\"，原文 cognitive functioning 只说\"有\n助于认知功能\"，并未承诺恢复。\nC) 无中生有\nClosely monitor the patients' movements and conditions around the clock. 中\naround the clock（24 小时不间断）与 closely monitor movements 都未在原文出\n现，原文仅说\"远程监护\"且未规定时长。\nD) ✓ 正确\nallowing family members or doctors to remotely monitor patients or Skype them ↔\nFacilitate communication between patients and doctors or family members.\nenhance communication and decrease feelings of loneliness ↔\nFacilitate communication between patients and doctors or family members.\nD 用上位词 communication 与 facilitate 收束原文两处下位动作（remotely\nmonitor、Skype、enhance communication），与预判完全契合。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What is one special feature of the robot Actroid F?", "options": ["A) It interacts with patients just like a human companion.", "B) It operates quietly without patients realizing its presence.", "C) It likes to engage in everyday conversations with patients.", "D) It uses body language even more effectively than words."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is one special feature of the robot Actroid F? 问\"一个独有特征\"，是对客观\n属性的询问。\n【拆句】What is one special feature of the robot Actroid F? 主系表结构，one special feature\n是表语前置的疑问主语，of 短语点明所属对象。无难点。\n【定位】On the less cute and more scary side there is Actroid F, which is so human-like that some\npatients may not know the difference. This conversational robot companion has cameras in its\neyes, which allow it to track patients and use appropriate facial expressions and body\nlanguage in its interactions.\nActroid F 没那么可爱、反倒有点吓人，它极度拟人化，以至于一些病人都分不出真假。这\n位\"会聊天的机器人陪伴者\"眼里嵌着摄像头，可以追踪病人并在互动中使用恰当的面部表情\n和肢体语言。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nso human-like that some patients may not know the difference ↔\nIt interacts with patients just like a human companion.\nThis conversational robot companion has cameras in its eyes, which allow it to track patients\nand use appropriate facial expressions and body language in its interactions.\n↔ It interacts with patients just like a human companion.\nA 把\"human-like + conversational companion + interactions\"三处信息收敛为一\n句\"like a human companion 互动\"，与预判完全契合。\nB) 偷换概念\nIt operates quietly without patients realizing its presence. 把\"病人难辨真假\"误读\n成\"病人没察觉\"，原文 may not know the difference 是\"分不清真假\"，不是\"没察觉\n存在\"。\nC) 过度推断\nIt likes to engage in everyday conversations with patients. 把 conversational（可\n对话的）夸大为 likes everyday conversations（喜欢日常闲聊），原文未表达机器\n人\"喜欢\"任何事，机器人也不具人格偏好。\nD) 无中生有\nIt uses body language even more effectively than words. 把\"使用面部表情和肢体语\n言\"夸大为\"肢体语言比词语更有效\"，原文未做任何\"肢体语言 vs 词语\"的比较。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What can we infer from the last paragraph?", "options": ["A) Doctors and surgeons will soon be laid off.", "B) The robotics industry will soon take off.", "C) Robots will not make nurses redundant.", "D) Collaboration will not replace competition."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （推断题）\n【判型】题干 What can we infer from the last paragraph? 含 infer，明确要求基于末段做一步\n推断。\n【拆句】What can we infer from the last paragraph? What 作 infer 的宾语前置，can we infer\n是情态动词疑问，from the last paragraph 是来源状语。结构清楚。\n【定位】Instead, they perform routine and laborious tasks, freeing nurses up to attend to patients with\nimmediate needs. This is one industry where it seems the integration of robots will lead to\ncollaboration, not replacement.\n相反，机器人做的是常规而繁重的活儿，让护士腾出手照顾急需照护的病人。机器人融入这\n一行业，看来带来的将是合作而非取代。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nDoctors and surgeons will soon be laid off. 与原文 robot doctors and surgeons\nmay not be far off 正相反——原文说\"机器人医生/外科医生可能不远了\"，是人替换为\n机器人；而且整段强调合作而非替代，谁也不会被裁。\nB) 以偏概全\nThe robotics industry will soon take off. 把原文 This is one industry（指护理这一\n行业）扩大为\"整个机器人产业\"。原文只评护理行业用机器人的前景，不评机器人产业\n自身。\nC) ✓ 正确\nThis is one industry where it seems the integration of robots will lead to collaboration, not\nreplacement.\n↔ Robots will not make nurses redundant.\nfreeing nurses up to attend to patients with immediate needs ↔\nRobots will not make nurses redundant.\nC 把\"not replacement\"用 make…redundant 这一英式习语反向表达，词面与原文最远\n但语义最近，是推断题正确项的典型样态。\nD) 偷换概念\nCollaboration will not replace competition. 把原文 collaboration, not replacement\n中的 replacement（取代）偷换成 competition（竞争），并把 collaboration 的对立\n面强行改换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}]});
